- id: CREDIBLE-173
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: capability inventory self-audit — catch negative-space blind spots before manual rediscovery
  status: done
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  description: |
    Context (operator conversation, 2026-07-29, no code written): walked through what
    ChumpOS is vs. a pile of tools. Grounded in docs/CODEBASE_REALITY_MAP.md's §2
    capability map (8 named areas: multi-agent coordination, LLM infra, AI-native
    work/planning, autonomous CI/ship firewall, agent memory, observability, dev
    surfaces, external-repo automation) and its §3 "negative space" — a SEPARATE
    manual sweep found ~8 more real clusters (cognitive-architecture/consciousness
    ~7.9K LOC, eval/adversarial harness, tool-governance/WASM sandbox, PR-review
    intelligence, omni-channel messaging, skills registry, HITL/operator-modeling,
    device control) that the tidy 8-area scan missed entirely, because "a capability
    scan finds what it's told to look for."
    
    The operator's point: Chump's own tools need to be good enough that they don't
    miss — right now the negative-space catch was a one-time manual sweep
    (2026-07-05 dated), not a mechanism. Nothing stops the map from rotting as new
    modules land, or the next capability audit from tunnel-visioning on whatever
    buckets it started with and missing a whole new category again.
    
    Rough shape (per two-phase decomposition doctrine — decompose at claim time,
    not now):
    (a) Turn the §2 + §3 bucket list into a structured registry (yaml/json) mapping
        module/crate path glob -> capability bucket name.
    (b) A scan step diffs the actual module/crate tree (src/**, crates/*/src/**)
        against that registry and flags LOC clusters above a threshold with no
        bucket attribution — i.e. detects the next "we didn't know this existed"
        category before a human has to manually rediscover it.
    (c) Wire into an EXISTING cadence (quartermaster shelfware-audit or harvester
        arsenal refresh) rather than standing up a new one-off daemon —
        Rust-first / no-duplicate-daemon doctrine (META-063/064) applies.
    (d) Emits an ambient event kind on drift found; does NOT auto-file a gap
        (MISSION-045 "signals are not work" anti-bloat doctrine — a flagged
        blind spot is a SIGNAL an operator/curator triages, not automatic backlog).
    (e) This is the CREDIBLE-090 signal≠outcome doctrine applied reflexively to
        Chump's own self-inventory: the Reality Map's claimed capability map should
        not be trusted as complete just because it exists and has a gap ID — same
        discipline the doc itself demands of every other module.
    
    Related, do not duplicate: MISSION-067 (kernel ABI, blocked) and INFRA-3287
    (monolith decompose, done 2026-07-21) are the MECHANISTIC unlocks that turn the
    8+8 areas into one coherent OS. This gap is the CREDIBILITY backstop so that
    "coherence" claim doesn't quietly regress the way fleet ship/lease counters
    have lied before. See docs/CODEBASE_REALITY_MAP.md and
    docs/strategy/CREWAI_PRIOR_ART_2026-07-28.md (OS-vs-framework invariant) for
    full context — this gap exists so the synthesis isn't lost to a chat transcript.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a structured capability-bucket registry exists (module/crate glob -> bucket name) covering all 16 areas currently named in docs/CODEBASE_REALITY_MAP.md §2+§3
    - a scan (script or existing cadence hook) diffs the real module/crate tree against the registry and flags unattributed LOC clusters above a defined threshold
    - the flag emits an ambient event kind (per EVENT_REGISTRY.yaml discipline) rather than auto-filing a gap, per MISSION-045
    - docs/CODEBASE_REALITY_MAP.md's provenance note is updated to reference the mechanized check instead of describing only a one-time manual sweep
  closed_date: '2026-07-30'
  closed_pr: 3407

- id: CREDIBLE-174
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: enforce reviewer-role write-tool restriction at the tool level, not just prompt instruction
  status: done
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Context (2026-07-29, operator comparison against github.com/ehippy/built): built is
    a tiny (1-day-old, 0-star, MIT) LLM-staffed Kanban pipeline — PM -> Developer ->
    Tester -> Reviewer -> Deployer. Its Reviewer stage has NO bash/write/edit tools at
    all -- a hard architectural constraint, not a prompt instruction ("don't edit
    files"). It can only judge the diff and hand back a verdict; it structurally
    cannot "fix" what it's reviewing, which means a compromised or sloppy Reviewer
    prompt can never silently patch over its own finding.
    
    Chump's PR-review-intelligence cluster (pr_triage/explain/fix_clippy/ac_coverage/
    coupling, diff_review_tool.rs -- see docs/CODEBASE_REALITY_MAP.md SS2 "Developer
    surfaces" / "PR-review intelligence ~5K LOC") does not currently enforce this
    separation at the tool level as far as this audit found -- review-class agent
    invocations get the same tool surface as implementation-class ones, gated only by
    prompt instruction (self-discipline), which is weaker than a structural guarantee.
    
    Why this matters for Chump specifically: unlike built's fixed single-project
    pipeline, Chump's fleet runs review-adjacent work (curator PR audits, code-review
    skill, bot-merge preflight judgment calls) at much higher volume and autonomy --
    the credibility discipline this repo already applies everywhere else
    (CREDIBLE-090 signal!=outcome, "verify before believing") argues for the same
    hardening here: a review verdict should be structurally incapable of being the
    thing that also silently patched the code it was judging.
    
    Secondary data point worth carrying, not a separate action item: built's
    `auto_main` deploy mode is a working zero-human-touch merge path in a system
    built in ~1 day. Chump's own mission scoreboard (docs/MISSION.md (C) THE BINARY)
    still reports NO on a zero-human-touch PR merged in repairman29/BEAST-MODE,
    despite orders of magnitude more infrastructure. Not a knock -- a narrow,
    single-project pipeline reaching that milestone before a general-purpose
    coordination OS does is a real, humbling comparandum for anyone tempted to
    credit ChumpOS's scale of investment as proof it's closer to done.
    
    Rough shape (not pre-sliced, per two-phase decomposition doctrine):
    (a) identify which review-class code paths currently share a tool registry with
        implementation-class agent calls (likely tool_policy/middleware/routing per
        the Reality Map's SS3 "Tool-governance + WASM sandbox ~6K LOC" cluster)
    (b) define a "review-only" tool profile (read/search/comment, no write/edit/bash)
        and wire it as the enforced ceiling for reviewer-class dispatches, not an
        opt-in
    (c) this is a hardening/trust gap, not a missing-feature gap -- scope small,
        don't let it balloon into a general tool-policy redesign
  acceptance_criteria:
    - review-class agent tool profiles are enforced structurally (no write/edit/bash tool availability) rather than by prompt instruction alone, for at least the primary PR-review-intelligence code path
    - a test proves a reviewer-class dispatch cannot invoke a write/edit/bash tool even if instructed to
    - no regression to existing review-quality (pr_triage/fix_clippy/ac_coverage) functionality that legitimately needs read/search access
  notes: |
    Structural mechanism built (tool_policy::review_class_dispatch_active + tool_middleware gate, 5 tests) in worktree /tmp/chump-credible-174, uncommitted -- see CREDIBLE-181 for the finding that no real call site currently exists to wire it into. Do not close to done until CREDIBLE-181 lands (mechanism-only != AC1 met).
  closed_date: '2026-07-30'
  closed_pr: 3423

