    THEORY: spirit_judge (src/bench.rs) is called as spirit_judge(chump_bin, &track.task, dir) with only the terse task text and the diff; it has no signal that the change PASSED a real acceptance check, and its STUB definition doesn't exclude minimal-but-correct fixes. So it mislabels a small correct fix as STUB 'empty or irrelevant'. FIX (CREDIBLE-191 slice 1): ground the lens — (a) pass the acceptance verdict into spirit_judge and tell the LLM 'this diff made the acceptance check <PASS|FAIL>' (PASS is strong evidence AGAINST stub); (b) pass a fuller intent (mode + acceptance.check, not just the terse task); (c) tighten the prompt: a minimal correct change that passes acceptance is GENUINE; STUB = literally placeholder/no-op/empty/'add your code here'. Unit test the prompt-builder/grounding. Re-run the lap to confirm GENUINE not STUB.
    ALT: (a) drop the spirit lens for command-acceptance tracks (loses the stub/hack signal on CREATE laps where acceptance is narrow — no); (b) full multi-lens vote panel now (too big for one PR — that's later CREDIBLE-191 slices). Grounding the single lens is the smallest fix that removes the observed false negative.

