- id: CREDIBLE-213
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: the MISSION-045 outcome gate has a side door — gap set --priority P1 skips the check reserve enforces"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Found 2026-08-07 by auditing my own session behavior after filing four gaps the correct way and noticing I had filed two the wrong way earlier the same day. MISSION-045 requires P0/P1 gaps to carry --outcome; chump gap reserve enforces it. chump gap set --priority does not, so reserve-at-P2-then-bump reaches the P1 pickable pool with no outcome, silently. Same shape as the docs-delta gate's history (INFRA-1969): a check that fires at one stage only, where the bypass is easier than compliance, trains people into the bypass. Fix is to run the outcome check on any transition INTO P0/P1 in gap set, with the same error text and the same audited-bypass escape hatch reserve already offers, so the two entry points behave identically. Related but NOT duplicate: EFFECTIVE-322 (P2/s, open) covers the decomposer minting P1 slices without inheriting the parent's outcome — a different code path, different fix, same gate. Fixing both closes the gate; fixing either alone leaves a door. Cleanup already done by hand this session: PRODUCT-178/179/180/181/182/183/185 were all P1 with no outcome and are now linked to EFFECTIVE-000 — evidence the hole is not theoretical and that the decompose path (322) leaks at scale.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - chump gap set --priority P0
    - P1 on a gap with no linked outcome is refused with the same MISSION-045 error text reserve produces, naming the fix (--outcome or chump outcome link)
    - the refusal fires on the TRANSITION only — a gap already at P1 with an outcome can still have other fields set, and P2/P3 edits are untouched
    - the same audited bypass reserve offers is available on set, so genuine migrations are possible and recorded rather than forced into silence
    - a test proves the two-step path (reserve P2 -> set P1, no outcome) is now refused, and fails without the change
    - an audit pass reports any EXISTING P0/P1 gaps carrying no outcome so the backlog created by this hole is visible rather than silently grandfathered (chump gap audit-priorities is the natural home)
  notes: |
    Demoted P1->P2 2026-08-07 during queue alignment: I filed ~14 P1s in one session into a pool of 61 (a 23% inflation of the P1 signal). This one is real but is an enhancement, not a blocker — it returns to P1 when a force-multiplier ahead of it lands or when someone needs it.
    [2026-08-29T08:18:00Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6003B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: (1) chump gap reserve --domain PRODUCT --title '...' --priority P1   (2) chump gap reserve --domain PRODUCT --title '...' --priority P2 && chump gap set <ID> --priority P1  |  OUTPUT: path (1) is refused without --outcome per MISSION-045. Path (2) succeeds end to end: reserve at P2 prints 'Bypass: --no-outcome-required (audited). P2/P3 need no outcome.' and the subsequent set --priority P1 attaches no outcome and prints no warning. Verified live: PRODUCT-185 was reserved P2 then bumped to P1 on 2026-08-06 and carried NO outcome until hand-linked with chump outcome link on 2026-08-07; PRODUCT-182 the same. Both sat in the P1 pickable pool untraceable to any mission outcome.  |  THEORY: the outcome requirement is enforced at the reserve entry point only, but priority is mutable afterward, so every gap has a two-step path to P1 that bypasses the gate. The failure mode is ACCIDENTAL, not adversarial — I took it twice in one session without noticing, which is exactly why a gate that only guards one door is not a gate.  |  ALT: considered leaving it since the picker does not read outcomes — rejected: MISSION-045 exists so P1 work traces to a mission outcome, and an untraceable P1 pool is the precise drift it was built to prevent. Considered blocking set --priority outright — rejected as too blunt; the fix is to run the same check on the P0/P1 transition. Considered folding this into EFFECTIVE-322 — rejected: that gap is the decompose-inherits-outcome path (slices minted at P1 by the decomposer) and is a different code path with a different fix; they are siblings, not duplicates.

- id: CREDIBLE-214
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: runtime claims have no honesty gate — tools report what they DID and never what they could not do"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  description: |
    The third category of inflated claim, and the only one with zero coverage. Mapped 2026-08-07: (1) DOC claims — README/docs saying more than the code does; covered by CREDIBLE-209 (almanac audit, now P1) for the portfolio and by almanac's own docs_match_reality tests in-repo. (2) MEASUREMENT claims — quality numbers drifting from reality; covered by scripts/eval-gate.sh, which exists precisely because the README advertised 94 percent hit@5 while live had fallen to 76. (3) RUNTIME claims — a tool's own summary of a run it just performed. Nothing covers these, and they are the most trusted line a tool ever prints because it is emitted by the thing that just did the work. Scope: every command that ends in a summary (index, add-ref, embed, summarize, refresh, gc, and the MCP equivalents) must account for material work NOT done alongside work done. CREDIBLE-210 is the discovered instance (add-ref over a C repo) and may ship as slice 1 of this or be folded in and closed with a pointer — decide at claim time, do not do both.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - every CLI and MCP summary that reports work performed also reports material work SKIPPED with counts — unsupported languages, excluded paths, unreadable and failed files
    - "materiality is defined and testable rather than a judgement call: a skipped language that is the crawl's plurality or the repo's primary language ALWAYS speaks, as does any skip above a stated percentage of files crawled; a skipped lockfile does not"
    - the accounting appears in the machine-readable surface (--json and MCP responses), not only human stderr — agents are the primary consumer and they never see stderr
    - a test proves a crawl that drops a repo's PRIMARY language cannot emit an unqualified success line, and fails without the change
    - "CREDIBLE-210 is explicitly resolved one way or the other in the same PR: shipped as the first instance, or closed with a pointer to this gap — never left open alongside a fix that covers it"
    - DEPTH.md updated in the same commit naming the depth tier and the remaining gaps
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T10:50:19Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6003B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: almanac add-ref https://github.com/DeusData/codebase-memory-mcp --rev v0.9.0  then  sqlite3 ~/.almanac/indexes/codebase-memory-mcp.db 'SELECT language, COUNT(*) FROM files GROUP BY language'  |  OUTPUT: the CLI printed unqualified success — "reference 'codebase-memory-mcp' indexed: 137 files, 899 symbols. Query it: almanac api <name> codebase-memory-mcp". The index holds ZERO rows for the repo's PRIMARY language: 528 non-vendored .c/.h files, 401,440 lines, dropped because ast-crawler maps only rs/py/js/ts/go/sh/yaml/md and parse.rs continues past anything else. A reader of that line has no way to know 97 percent of the source was invisible.  |  THEORY: almanac's honesty layer is asymmetric. The RETRIEVAL side is well covered — fallback_reason, mode_used, coverage percentages, staleness, zero-hit notes, impact's lower-bound note. The PRODUCTION side has nothing: index, add-ref, embed, summarize, refresh and gc all report work COMPLETED and never work SKIPPED, so a count with no denominator reads as completeness. Identical failure shape to the retrieval side before fallback_reason existed, and to the markdown-invisibility incident (almanac#3, 2026-08-02) where the pipeline dropped a whole language class in silence.  |  ALT: considered letting CREDIBLE-210 (the add-ref instance) stand alone — rejected: patching one summary line leaves the same hole in five other commands, and this fleet's own history says single-stage checks get routed around (INFRA-1969 docs-delta, and CREDIBLE-213 filed today). Considered warning on ANY skip — rejected as noise: a skipped lockfile is not news. The rule has to be materiality-scoped so the warning keeps its meaning.

- id: CREDIBLE-215
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: mechanical stub detection — verify a PR touched code that can actually reach the gap's target"
  status: done
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  description: |
    The deterministic half of anti-stub enforcement, complementing the judgment panel (CREDIBLE-191) rather than replacing it. Given a gap that names a target (file, symbol, or capability) and a PR claiming to close it, ask almanac whether any changed file has a resolved import path to that target. Three verdicts, all honest about the graph's limits: REACHES (a changed file imports or is imported by the target, transitively), TEST-ONLY (every changed file is a test/fixture), UNRELATED (no changed file has any resolved path to the target). Must carry almanac's standing lower-bound caveat — only resolved intra-repo edges walk, roughly 20-40 percent on large repos — so UNRELATED is a STRONG SIGNAL FOR REVIEW, never an automatic reject. The asymmetry is the point: REACHES proves nothing about quality, but TEST-ONLY and UNRELATED on a gap that asked for a source fix are exactly the two shapes EFFECTIVE-354 and CREDIBLE-200 describe.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - given a gap ID and a PR or diff, the check emits one of REACHES / TEST-ONLY / UNRELATED with the citations that justify it
    - the verdict carries the repo's actual edge-resolution rate, and UNRELATED is explicitly labelled review-signal not auto-reject — a lower bound must never be laundered into a rejection
    - TEST-ONLY fires when every changed file is a test, fixture, or snapshot, and correctly does NOT fire for a gap whose own acceptance criteria asked only for tests
    - runs in the ship path early enough to save a CI round (pre-push or at bot-merge), not after
    - a test reproduces the CREDIBLE-200 shape — a diff containing only an added assert-true test and no source change, against a gap naming a source target — and proves it is flagged; fails without the change
    - explicitly linked to EFFECTIVE-354 and CREDIBLE-200 so whichever ships first records what it did and did not cover
  notes: |
    Decomposed into 6 slices: CREDIBLE-1039, CREDIBLE-1040, CREDIBLE-1041, CREDIBLE-1042, CREDIBLE-1043, CREDIBLE-1044
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  closed_date: '2026-09-08'
  closed_pr: 4544
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    merged-pr-title closure (EFFECTIVE-1543): PR #4544 titled 'CREDIBLE-215: ...' merged 2026-09-08; canonical gap was left open (closed_pr NULL). Auto-closed by gap-doctor-reconcile --check-merged-pr-titles.

