- id: CREDIBLE-234
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: eight workspace advisor agents are Claude-only prompts — the harness-neutrality law is violated one directory above the repo that states it"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  description: |
    Make the workspace advisory crew reachable by anything. The pattern is already proven twice: posse (bot.mjs / surveyor.mjs / crawl.mjs / targets.json / report-holler.mjs / STRANGER_AGENT.md) and privateer (voyage.mjs / charter.json / VOYAGER.md / charts/, created 2026-08-08). In both, the deterministic work is a plain executable, config is data, the AI tier is a prose document, and outputs are committed files — so a cron, a shell, or any harness can run it, and the return survives the session. Scope: audit the eight, decide per agent whether it needs a harness-neutral capability (most do — market-scout and frontier-correspondent especially, since both should have a cadence and neither can have one today), and carve those out with a thin .claude adapter left behind pointing at them, exactly as INFRA-1918/1922/1923/1924/1925 did inside chump. Not every agent needs a repo; some may just need a script plus a doc in workspace-docs. The deliverable is that no advisory capability requires a specific harness in a specific directory.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "every one of the eight workspace agents is triaged with a written decision: carve out to a harness-neutral capability, fold into an existing repo, or retire — and the reason is recorded per agent"
    - for each one carved out, the capability is invokable from a plain shell with no Claude Code present, and a thin .claude/agents adapter remains that points at it (the INFRA-1918 pattern, not a rewrite)
    - market-scout and frontier-correspondent specifically become cadence-capable — a cron can run them and their output lands somewhere durable, because 'the ideas agent has no cadence' is the load-bearing symptom
    - ~/Projects/.claude/README.md is created stating the same adapter law chump's own README states, so the workspace level stops being the unaudited exception
    - a test or documented receipt proves one carved-out agent runs headless with no harness present
    - the 89 opportunity dossiers are traceable to a capability that can regenerate them, or explicitly marked as a one-time human-triggered artifact
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T11:04:15Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6003B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: ls ~/Projects/.claude/agents/ ; cat ~/Projects/chump/.claude/README.md ; ls ~/Projects/almanac/.claude/  |  OUTPUT: eight agents (chief-engineer, market-scout, frontier-correspondent, infra-cartographer, quartermaster, release-auditor, herald, vercel-bosun) exist ONLY as .md prompt files in ~/Projects/.claude/agents/ with no harness-neutral capability behind any of them. Chump's own .claude/README.md states the law verbatim: 'This directory is NOT where Chump capabilities live... Every file under .claude/skills/ and .claude/agents/ MUST be a thin wrapper. The capability itself MUST live somewhere harness-neutral so any agent (Claude Code, opencode-bigpickle, codex, Aider, goose, manual operator) can use it.' Confirmed reachability failure live 2026-08-08: a session rooted at ~/Projects/almanac cannot dispatch market-scout at all — the agent roster does not include it, and ~/Projects/almanac/.claude/ has no agents dir to bridge from.  |  THEORY: the law was written for and enforced INSIDE chump — INFRA-1918/1922/1923/1924/1925 each productized a curator role as script PLUS thin adapter, correctly. The workspace level was never audited, so the portfolio's entire advisory crew is invisible to cron, to every non-Claude harness, and to any session opened in the wrong directory. The 89 opportunity dossiers are that crew's output; nothing can regenerate or refresh them without a human opening Claude Code in one specific folder. This is why market-scout and frontier-correspondent have no cadence, no inbox, and no destination.  |  ALT: considered leaving them as prompts since they are advisory and a human triggers them anyway — rejected: 'a human must be in the right directory' is exactly the single point of failure the RESILIENT pillar names, and it is the mechanism by which the outward-facing chairs stayed empty. Considered moving them into chump/.claude/agents/ — rejected: that relocates the violation without fixing it. The proven pattern is posse, and now repairman29/privateer built 2026-08-08: a real repo, executable plain-Node entry points, config as data, the AI tier as a DOCUMENT, artifacts committed.

- id: CREDIBLE-235
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: NATS has no auth — anyone reaching the tailnet can publish forged events into chump.events.*"
  status: wontfix
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  description: |
    Add authentication to the fleet's event bus. Scope proportionate to a single-operator tailnet: a token or user/password in the NATS server config, credentials supplied by env the same way provider keys already are, and every publisher/subscriber updated — scripts/coord/broadcast.sh, crates/chump-coord, the mesh-worker consumer, chumpbar, and anything else subscribing to chump.events.*. The migration is the risky part: an auth flip that misses one consumer silently stops that consumer from seeing events, which in this fleet looks exactly like a healthy-but-quiet organ (cf the curator dead 12 days). So the acceptance criteria demand an inventory of subscribers BEFORE the flip and a verified event round-trip AFTER, per consumer.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - NATS requires a credential; an unauthenticated TCP connect can no longer publish or subscribe, proven by a test that attempts both and is refused
    - every publisher and subscriber is inventoried BEFORE the flip (broadcast.sh, chump-coord, mesh-worker, chumpbar, and whatever else grep finds) and each one is verified to still send AND receive after — a missed consumer goes silent, which is indistinguishable from healthy in this fleet
    - credentials come from env/config the way provider keys already do, never argv (RESILIENT-173 already fixed that class once) and never a literal in a committed file
    - "a rollback is written down before the flip: how to restore an unauthenticated bus quickly if a consumer cannot be updated in time"
    - the ambient.jsonl file-based path still works as the degradation fallback, so an auth failure degrades to file-based events rather than a silent fleet
  notes: |
    PREMISE REFUTED 2026-08-08, same session it was filed. I claimed 'NATS has no auth — anyone reaching the tailnet can publish forged events.' FALSE. /etc/nats/nats.conf carries authorization{user,password} on BOTH the main listener and leafnodes; the INFO banner advertises auth_required:true; and an unauthenticated PUB is refused with -ERR 'Authorization Violation' (tested). Config perms are 600 root:root — correct. My error: I read the first 160 bytes of the banner and concluded 'no auth', but NATS always sends INFO BEFORE authentication, and auth_required sits past where I stopped reading. Fourth incomplete-check conclusion of the day, and the same shape as declaring four repos lost from a case-sensitive glob. WHAT REMAINS REAL, and much smaller than this gap: (1) the shared password is now exposed in a session transcript because I cat'd the config — it needs rotating (filed separately); (2) one static secret is shared between the main listener and leafnodes rather than per-service credentials; (3) no TLS, which is defensible on a tailnet. Closing wontfix rather than reopening reworded, so the false claim stays visible as the record.
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: exec 3<>/dev/tcp/100.101.188.30/4222 ; head -c 160 <&3  |  OUTPUT: INFO {"server_id":"NCZNDMEFV3Q6OEAHOOSZ4TBFNIQB5J3CDCOQTTHWTFNUV3RHI2PSWO56","server_name":"helsinki-chump","version":"2.14.3","proto":1,...} — a raw TCP connect gets the server banner with no credential presented. Until 2026-08-08 this was reachable from the OPEN INTERNET (see RESILIENT-251); it is now tailnet-only, but still unauthenticated within that boundary.  |  THEORY: chump-coord publishes WorkEnvelopes and the fleet's INTENT/HANDOFF/STUCK/DONE/WARN/ALERT/FEEDBACK broadcasts over chump.events.* (scripts/coord/broadcast.sh, crates/chump-coord EVENTS_SUBJECT). An unauthenticated bus means any process that can route to the tailnet can not only SUBSCRIBE (read fleet telemetry, work assignments, agent state) but PUBLISH — forge a DONE, inject a fake ALERT, or assign work. That is a fleet-integrity problem, not a confidentiality one, and it is exactly the class the reality-check gate exists to catch downstream rather than upstream.  |  ALT: considered treating the firewall as sufficient — rejected: the fleet's own doctrine is that a single point of failure is a defect, and a bus that drives agent behaviour should not be defended by exactly one iptables rule. Considered full mTLS/nkeys — likely over-scoped for a single-operator tailnet; a token or user/password in the NATS config with credentials distributed the same way provider keys already are (~/.chump/providers.env, launchd EnvironmentVariables) is proportionate and testable.

- id: CREDIBLE-236
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: rotate the NATS bus password — I exposed it in a session transcript"
  status: open
  priority: P1
  effort: xs
  description: |
    Operator action. Generate a new secret on helsinki (openssl rand -hex 32), replace it in /etc/nats/nats.conf for BOTH the authorization block and the leafnodes block, restart nats.service, then update every consumer that authenticates to the bus. The consumer list is the risky part and is the same inventory RESILIENT-251 needs: scripts/coord/broadcast.sh, crates/chump-coord, the mesh-worker loop, chumpbar, and anything else grep finds subscribing to chump.events.*. A missed consumer stops receiving events silently. Consider per-service credentials rather than one shared secret while the file is open anyway.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a new secret is in place for both the main listener and leafnodes, and the old one is refused — proven by attempting auth with the old credential and getting Authorization Violation
    - every consumer is inventoried and verified to both PUBLISH and RECEIVE after the rotation; a consumer that cannot be updated is named rather than left broken
    - "the new secret never appears in a transcript, a commit, or argv — it goes only into the 600 root:root config and whatever env the consumers already read"
    - the ambient.jsonl file-based fallback is confirmed still working, so an auth mismatch degrades to file-based events instead of a silent fleet
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: ssh helsinki 'sudo cat /etc/nats/nats.conf'  |  OUTPUT: the file contents, including authorization{user: chump, password: <64-hex>} for both the main listener and leafnodes, were printed into a Claude Code session transcript on 2026-08-08 while auditing the box. File perms on disk are correct (600 root:root) — the exposure is entirely the transcript.  |  THEORY: same class as the tvly-dev key pasted earlier the same day. A credential that has appeared in a transcript, session export, or log must be treated as compromised regardless of how well-permissioned the file is, because transcripts persist in storage nobody audits. The password is shared between the main listener and the leafnode listener, so one rotation covers both — and every consumer that authenticates with it must be updated in the same pass or it goes silent, which in this fleet is indistinguishable from healthy (cf the curator dead 12 days).  |  ALT: considered treating it as low-risk because the bus is now firewalled and tailnet-only — rejected: shop rule 4 is revoke-before-publish and the fleet has a documented history of exactly this reasoning going wrong. Considered rotating it myself — refused: I do not handle credentials, and generating plus distributing this one is the operator's action.

- id: CREDIBLE-237
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CI gates assert code LOCATION not behaviour: one refactor tripped 5 path-pinned greps, and one of them then passed vacuously"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "MEASURED 2026-08-08 on PR #3499 (INFRA-1965), which moves the 6,079-LOC gap command group from src/main.rs to src/commands/gap.rs. FIVE independent gates failed, none because anything broke — all because they grep a hardcoded path: test-gap-impact-rating.sh (FLEET-048) 'main.rs: gap rate subcommand missing'; test-open-pr-dup-detection.sh (INFRA-1982) 'expected >= 2 gap_reserve_similarity_warn in main.rs, found 0'; test-gap-audit-ac-open.sh (INFRA-936) 'INFRA-936 not found in src/main.rs'; test-stale-binary-ship-blocked.sh (INFRA-825) 'src/main.rs does not call fail_if_stale_for_destructive'. Every symbol was present in src/commands/gap.rs the whole time"
    - THE FIFTH IS THE DANGEROUS ONE AND IT DID NOT FAIL — it passed. test-stale-binary-ship-blocked.sh Test 3 asserts gap ship --update-yaml is NOT wired to the staleness guard (ZERO-WASTE-020 removed the write it protected). Pointed at src/main.rs after the move it greps for text that is no longer in that file at all, so it passes VACUOUSLY. A re-introduced unguarded destructive bulk-YAML write would now sail through a green check. A negative assertion pinned to a path silently becomes a no-op the moment the code moves, and nothing reports it
    - "SO THE FAILURE MODE IS TWO-SIDED: positive assertions false-FAIL on a refactor (noisy, self-announcing, cheap); negative assertions false-PASS (silent, permanent, and exactly the case a guard exists for). The noisy half is what gets noticed; the quiet half is what costs something"
    - "SWEEP THE REST. Four were found only because one PR happened to move code they pointed at. Grep scripts/ci/ for hardcoded src/*.rs paths and classify each: does it assert behaviour (fine) or location (fix)? Report the count — the useful deliverable here is knowing how many more are one refactor away from lying"
    - "FIX PATTERN, already applied to the four in PR #3499: resolve the dispatch source at runtime (new home first, old path as fallback) so the gate passes either side of a move. Better where practical: assert against the built binary's behaviour (chump gap <sub> --help) instead of grepping source at all — a grep for a symbol name cannot distinguish 'wired' from 'mentioned in a comment'"
    - "GUARD AGAINST THE VACUOUS-PASS CLASS SPECIFICALLY: any negative assertion (grep -q ... && fail) must first prove its search target EXISTS somewhere, else the test is asserting nothing. That precondition is what would have caught INFRA-825 Test 3 automatically"
    - "Filed from the fix session, not speculative: all four repairs and the vacuous-pass analysis are in PR #3499's commit 'unpin four CI gates from src/main.rs'"
  notes: |
    Decomposed into 9 slices: CREDIBLE-1222, CREDIBLE-1223, CREDIBLE-1224, CREDIBLE-1225, CREDIBLE-1226, CREDIBLE-1227, CREDIBLE-1228, CREDIBLE-1229, CREDIBLE-1230
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: MISSION-010

- id: CREDIBLE-238
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: exclude R&D from the mission-ship ratio or the 2/3 scaling gate becomes clearable by experimenting"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    One-line semantics, deliberately its own gap because losing it is silent and expensive. R&D counts for pillar BALANCE and RESTOCK (so the curator can see it starving) and is EXCLUDED from the mission-ship ratio in gate 2 (so the scaling gate cannot be cleared by experimenting). Both behaviours asserted, not assumed. Depends on META-329 landing the pillar first.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a merged R&D gap does NOT increment the mission-ship ratio numerator, proven by a test that computes the ratio with and without an R&D merge in the window
    - the same R&D gap DOES appear in pillar inventory and pillar_coverage, so balance_restock can still detect starvation
    - the scoreboard's gate-2 line states that R&D is excluded, so a reader is not left inferring it from code
    - a comment at the exclusion site says WHY, citing the gameable-gate reasoning — an unexplained exclusion gets 'cleaned up' by a future refactor
  depends_on: [META-329]
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: scripts/dev/mission-scoreboard.sh ; head -22 src/mission_grade.rs  |  OUTPUT: gate 2 reads 'Mission-ship ratio (24h): 15/28 (target >= 2/3 — mission-linked merges / total)', up from 5/18 on 2026-08-05. mission_grade.rs (INFRA-599) computes per-pillar counts by gap-title prefix and emits kind=mission_grade every 30 min via launchd.  |  THEORY: R&D's output is frequently a NO-GO — a kill counts as a successful run under the pillar's inverted success axis. If R&D merges count as mission-linked ships, the fleet can clear the 2/3 scaling gate by running experiments instead of shipping mission work, and gate 2 is the single number that says whether the factory has earned the right to point outward. That number is currently the operator's stated exit condition for factory-first sequencing, so corrupting it would remove the only objective brake on going outward too early. The six existing NO-GOs prove the volume is real, not hypothetical.  |  ALT: considered handling it inside META-329's refactor — rejected and deliberately split out: a broad mechanical 37-file change is exactly where a one-line metric semantic gets lost, and the failure is SILENT (the ratio just reads higher). Considered excluding R&D from all metrics — rejected: it must still count for balance and restock, or the curator cannot detect a starving pillar.

