- id: CREDIBLE-240
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: the capabilities catalog is 11 weeks stale and now INDEXED — almanac will serve May's fleet with a path:line receipt"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: s
  description: |
    Two deliverables. (1) REGENERATE: run scripts/ops/generate-capabilities-registry.sh against chump itself and commit the result, so the catalog describes the fleet that exists — 38 crates, 7 mcp-server crates, 6 wired servers including github and almanac. (2) KEEP IT HONEST, which matters more: schedule the generator (CI on merge to main is the natural trigger, since the catalog only changes when code does) AND make staleness self-reporting — the file carries generated_at, so anything reading it can compare against a threshold and say so. A catalog that can silently age three months is the same defect class as the curator exiting 78 for twelve days with nobody noticing. Bonus once fresh: it becomes the answer to 'which provider does this tool front', since the registry holds tools and privateer's charter.json holds providers and today neither references the other.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY.json regenerated against current chump and committed — mcp_tools includes github and almanac, crate_apis reflects 38 crates, and calculator is either present-and-wired or dropped
    - the generator runs on a trigger (CI on merge to main, or a scheduled job) and commits when the content actually changes — no trigger means this recurs
    - "staleness is self-reporting: something compares generated_at against a threshold and says so out loud where a reader will see it, so the next three-month drift surfaces in days"
    - a test or check proves a deliberately-backdated generated_at is flagged, not silently trusted
    - the relationship to privateer/charter.json is noted in at least one direction, so 'which provider does this tool front' has an answer
  closed_date: '2026-08-09'
  closed_pr: 3542
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: python3 -c 'json.load(open(docs/CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY.json))' ; ls -d crates/*/ crates/mcp-servers/*/ ; grep -rl CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY.json .github/workflows/  |  OUTPUT: generated_at=2026-05-23T16:33:55Z, generator_version=dev — eleven weeks old. It claims 5 mcp_tools (filesystem, git, gaps, memory, calculator) and 17 crate_apis. Reality on 2026-08-08: 7 mcp-server crates, 6 wired in chump-mcp.json (github and almanac appear in neither list; calculator is wired nowhere), and 38 crates. FOUR scripts reference the file — scripts/ops/generate-capabilities-registry.sh (a working wrapper over scripts/dev/build-capabilities-registry.sh), scripts/dev/build-capabilities-registry.sh, plus a CI smoke test and a test — and grep across .github/workflows/ returns NOTHING. No workflow, no cron, no launchd job runs any of them.  |  THEORY: the generator was built for 'chump ingest <repo>' (INFRA-1729, Quartermaster) to produce a catalog for an ARBITRARY repo, and was never turned on chump itself. The file was generated once and committed. Until 2026-08-08 this was invisible and therefore harmless — JSON was unindexed. Indexing it (commit 8ffbef3, at the operator's request) converted a stale file into a CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER: almanac now serves May's capability list with a path:line citation, which is strictly worse than 'I don't know'. Almanac made the staleness visible; it did not cause it.  |  ALT: considered un-indexing JSON to remove the wrong answer — rejected: that hides the problem and throws away the catalogs that ARE current (posse/targets.json, privateer/charter.json, chump-mcp.json). Considered regenerating once by hand — rejected as the same one-shot that produced this: a catalog that can go stale silently is the curator-dead-12-days defect class, and the fix is the freshness gate, not another manual run.

- id: CREDIBLE-241
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: [almanac] non-git sources can never go stale because refresh has no HEAD to compare — and one is stamped with its parent repo's commit"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Two related defects found 2026-08-08 while auditing source freshness. (1) refresh() decides staleness by comparing git HEAD to indexed_commit. For a non-git directory head_commit() returns None, the match falls through to '_ => force', and the repo is re-indexed ONLY under --force. The hourly cron runs plain 'almanac refresh', so a non-git source is indexed once and never again. Three are registered today — fucked, twenty1, house-sit-form-helper — all added this session, all now permanently frozen unless someone remembers --force. (2) Worse and subtler: 'almanac refresh fucked' reports 'fresh 4a0315b...' — a commit hash, for a directory with no .git. Cause: head_commit runs 'git -C <path> rev-parse HEAD', which WALKS UP the tree. ~/Projects is itself a git repo, so every non-git child inherits the PARENT's HEAD as its indexed_commit. The consequence is not merely cosmetic: those children now re-index whenever ~/Projects commits, for reasons wholly unrelated to their own content, and report 'fresh' the rest of the time. Fix direction: detect the repo boundary (git rev-parse --show-toplevel and compare to the indexed root) and treat a mismatch as non-git; then give non-git sources a real change signal — a content digest or max-mtime over the crawl — so they can be honestly stale. Related: the discovery cron only finds git repos, which is why non-git dirs must be registered by hand (the opportunity-library blind spot) — this gap is that blind spot's other half, freshness rather than discovery.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "head_commit does not attribute an ancestor repo's HEAD to a non-git subdirectory: rev-parse --show-toplevel is compared against the indexed root and a mismatch yields None"
    - non-git sources get a real staleness signal (content digest or max-mtime over the crawl) so plain 'almanac refresh' can detect a change without --force
    - "a test covers the exact live shape: a non-git directory nested inside a git repo must NOT report the parent's commit and must NOT report 'fresh' after its files change"
    - the three currently-registered non-git sources re-index correctly after an edit, proven by editing one and running plain refresh
    - almanac repos or status shows which sources use which staleness mechanism, so 'this one cannot detect changes' is visible rather than inferred
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T08:33:50Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6003B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"

- id: CREDIBLE-242
  domain: CREDIBLE
  title: "CREDIBLE: [almanac] reference-repo citations carry no provenance — third-party code looks identical to ours"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    almanac add-ref clones a PUBLIC third-party repo and indexes it so agents can ground against a real API instead of inventing one — tokio is the documented example, and codebase-memory-mcp (MIT, 37.9k stars) was added 2026-08-08. The citations those produce are indistinguishable from the fleet's own: 'codebase-memory-mcp:internal/cbm/foo.c:42' looks exactly like 'chump:src/foo.rs:42'. Nothing in the response says the code is third-party, what licence governs it, or which revision it was pinned at. That matters more here than for ordinary search because almanac_api EXISTS to make agents copy the exact signature verbatim — the anti-hallucination lookup is also, structurally, a copy-this instruction. An agent lifting an implementation from an MIT or GPL reference has no signal that attribution is owed. The registry already knows origin_url for remote entries and add-ref already takes --rev, so most of the facts are in hand; they are simply not carried into the answer. Related but distinct: the four repairman29 clones under ~/.chump/external are deliberately NOT indexed (they duplicate registered sources and are mutable agent worktrees) — that is a coverage decision, this is a provenance one.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a hit from an add-ref'd repo is marked as external in both CLI and MCP output — an agent can tell third-party code from ours without knowing the slug
    - the citation carries origin URL, pinned rev, and licence where detectable (LICENSE file or SPDX header); undetectable licence says 'licence unknown', never blank
    - almanac_api specifically flags external results, since it is the surface whose whole purpose is 'copy this verbatim'
    - the registry records which entries came from add-ref versus local registration, so the distinction survives a re-index
    - a test asserts an external hit cannot render identically to a first-party hit
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T08:34:36Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6003B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000

