    RFC-rnd-pillar (shipped in PR #3526 earlier today) asserts that R&D 'runs outside the picker' because it has a hypothesis and ceiling rather than acceptance criteria. That is wrong on inspection: privateer's voyage.mjs writes 'What would count as treasure' and 'What would count as a chart' into every pending chart, and those two sections ARE specific testable acceptance criteria — machine-generated, which is the same move EFFECTIVE-386 (AC-writer, shipped 2026-08-07) makes for gaps. So R&D work can ride the existing gap pipeline: pick, claim, worktree, dispatch, ship. Building a parallel scheduler and dispatcher for it would be a fifth hand-rolled cascade of the shape the search voyage just catalogued (completion, embedding, TTS, STT). Left uncorrected, the RFC sends whoever implements the pillar down the wrong road. The admission-form claim was RIGHT and stays: a voyage is admitted on a hypothesis plus a spend ceiling, not on hand-written ACs — the tool generates them.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "the 'runs outside the picker' mechanic is replaced with the accurate version: a voyage's acceptance criteria are machine-generated by the tool, so R&D reuses the gap pipeline rather than a parallel dispatcher"
    - the correction cites EFFECTIVE-386 (AC-writer) as the same pattern arrived at independently
    - "the honest caveat is stated: a voyage filed as an external-repo gap inherits EFFECTIVE-353/354 (external execution currently stubs), so reusing the pipeline is a real test of that machinery and not a safe bet"
    - the admission form (hypothesis + spend ceiling, not hand-written ACs) is preserved — that part of the RFC was correct
    - no other RFC section is silently rewritten; the correction is marked as a correction with its date
