- id: EFFECTIVE-419
  domain: EFFECTIVE
  title: "EFFECTIVE: [almanac] the key discount trades catalog discoverability for ranking — a generic tools question buries the catalog"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    A tension I introduced on 2026-08-08 and have not resolved. Indexing JSON catalogs cost chump-breadth 0.10 fusion MRR at flat hit-rate, so  symbols are halved in keyword scoring (commit 8ffbef3, harder than the 25% heading discount because a key carries a name and no prose). That fixed ranking and cost discoverability. Measured the same day: 'mcp_tools filesystem git gaps memory' — a plainly catalog-shaped question — returns orchestrator main.rs, a webhook receiver, gap-store and highlight.js, and NOT docs/CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY.json, which holds 5,982 indexed symbols and is exactly the right answer. A targeted query ('CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY crate_apis event_kinds') returns it at rank 1. So the catalog is findable when you already know its name, which is the opposite of what a catalog is for. Possible directions, to be MEASURED not assumed: score a key by whether the query looks catalog-shaped rather than by a flat discount; boost keys whose file is small and dense (a registry) over keys in incidental config; or treat catalog files as a distinct kind from ordinary JSON config. Whatever is tried must run through all four gate suites including almanac-docs, since the flat discount is currently what holds chump-breadth's MRR up.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a generic catalog-shaped question ('what mcp tools does the fleet have', 'which cli commands exist') surfaces CAPABILITIES_REGISTRY.json in the top 5 — that exact query is the regression test and it must fail without the change
    - chump-breadth fusion MRR does not fall back toward the -0.10 the flat discount was introduced to fix; all four suites run before and after with numbers in the commit message
    - the mechanism chosen is stated with its reasoning, since the next person will otherwise read the discount as arbitrary
    - DEPTH.md's retrieve.rs row is updated — it currently records the key discount as measured-but-not-unit-tested, and whatever replaces it needs the same honesty
  depends_on: [CREDIBLE-240]
  notes: |
    Description note: one word was eaten by shell backtick expansion at filing time — the sentence reads "so  symbols are halved" and should read "so KEY-kind symbols are halved". The mechanism is the h.kind == "key" branch in crates/almanac-core/src/retrieve.rs, which halves the score of every JSON key symbol.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: EFFECTIVE-000

- id: EFFECTIVE-420
  domain: EFFECTIVE
  title: "EFFECTIVE: audit is the merge floor and its shards are imbalanced 6:1 — median PR could go 12.5 min to ~5"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  description: |
    Measured 2026-08-08 across the last 12 merged PRs and 3 Audit runs. Median open-to-merge is 12.5 min (min 7.5, max 780) and Audit is the floor on EVERY pr including doc-only ones, since the express lane skips the heavy Rust lanes but audit always runs. Two costs, both structural, neither requiring any change to WHAT is checked. (1) SHARD IMBALANCE: shards run in parallel so wall time is max-of-shards, and they are assigned by CATEGORY not by cost — shard-1 (cargo gates, needs cargo build) 7.6 min, shard-4 (cross-PR + security) 6.5, shard-3 (bash) 1.5, shard-2 (event-registry) 1.2. Total work 16.8 min over 4 runners; balanced that is 4.2 each. INFRA-2565 built this matrix targeting '14 min -> ~4-6 min' and landed at 7.6, so it half-achieved its own stated goal. (2) AGGREGATOR OVERHEAD: audit-required takes 4.5 min to assert that audit passed. It runs-on ubuntu-latest and does a full actions/checkout@v7 — a fresh runner provision plus a large-repo checkout — to read a needs-context conclusion it already has. Together the critical path is ~12.1 min; balanced shards plus a checkout-free aggregator projects to ~4.4. NOT batching: PRs already run concurrently and the merge queue already exists (INFRA-1377, done), so batching would save compute at the cost of 1:1 gap-to-PR traceability, blast-radius isolation and clean reverts — all three of which this fleet has active incidents about (CREDIBLE-233, CREDIBLE-215, revert_pr.rs).
  acceptance_criteria:
    - shards are balanced by MEASURED duration rather than category — rebalanced from observed timings, and max-of-shards drops below 5 min on a representative PR
    - "the balance is maintainable: something records per-shard timings so the next drift is visible, since category-assignment drifted to 6:1 without anyone noticing"
    - audit-required stops doing a full checkout to assert a status — no checkout, or a minimal one, taking under 1 min
    - "nothing changes about WHAT is audited: the same gates run, proven by comparing the gate list before and after"
    - "measured after: median open-to-merge on 10 subsequent PRs, reported in the closing comment — the claim is a time saving and it needs a receipt"
    - the express lane still applies (doc-only PRs skip the Rust lanes) and doc-only audit gets cheaper too, since audit is their entire cost
  notes: |
    MEASURED RECEIPT (2026-08-09). audit wall-clock is max-of-jobs; all four runs are on main.
    
      commit      shard1  shard4  audit-required   FLOOR
      3c4f16cb     476s    422s      297s          476s   <- baseline, before either change
      6bda4ec9     318s    259s        -           318s   <- after #3534 (CARGO_TARGET_DIR) only
      14d2f44e     289s    249s        -           289s   <- after #3532 (shared-key) also landed
      c20ebe12     281s    241s      284s          284s   <- current
    
      476s -> 284s = 192s (40%) off audit's floor, on every PR.
    
    Attribution: #3534 (stable target dir, so rust-cache can hold compiled artifacts)
    did the bulk — 476->318 = 158s. #3532's shared-key change added ~30s more by
    pointing audit at the 1159MB ci-fast-checks cache instead of its own 207MB one.
    
    NOTE on #3532: it merged at 02:41 despite my attempt to close it — the bot had
    already merged it. Its CHANGE turned out mildly beneficial, but its stated
    REASONING was false and that false claim is now a comment on main (audit.yml:118
    says shared-key ci-audit was written by NOTHING; five such caches existed). Filed
    separately to correct the comment — a wrong receipt in the tree is worse than no
    comment, since the next person will trust it.
    
    BOTTLENECK HAS MOVED: audit-required is now 284s vs shard1's 281s. Further shard
    rebalancing buys ~0 until audit-required is addressed. It runs three real test
    scripts (I nearly deleted its checkout earlier assuming it was vestigial — it is
    not). That is the next lever for anyone continuing the faster-merge work.
  closed_date: '2026-08-09'
  closed_pr: 3534
  outcome_id: EFFECTIVE-000

- id: EFFECTIVE-421
  domain: EFFECTIVE
  title: "EFFECTIVE: [almanac] index open + recently-merged PRs — agents cannot see work in flight"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  description: |
    Operator 2026-08-08: 'almanac needs to see planned/recent PRs too.' Almanac indexes COMMITTED code. It cannot see an open PR, so an agent has no way to answer the two questions that most directly waste fleet time: 'is someone already doing this' and 'what changed recently, and why'. almanac_why reaches merged history via git blame, but in-flight work is entirely invisible. Concretely: ZERO-WASTE-045 dedupes a new gap against the gap registry, and ZERO-WASTE-046 against fleet CODE, but neither sees that an open PR is already implementing the thing — so two agents can converge on the same work with one of them already halfway done. That is a bigger throughput win than any CI tuning, because it prevents work rather than accelerating it. Scope: index PR metadata per registered repo — number, title, body, state, branch, changed file paths, gap ID from the title, opened/merged timestamps — refreshed on the same cadence as the index. Changed FILE PATHS matter most: they make 'who else is touching this file' answerable, which is the collision question. Recently-merged PRs give the other half — a 'what changed here lately and why' view that pairs with almanac_why's blame. Deliberately metadata, not diffs: storing patches would balloon the index and duplicate git.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - open PRs for a registered repo are queryable with their changed file paths, so 'is anyone already touching src/foo.rs' has an answer
    - recently-merged PRs (a bounded window) are queryable with title, gap ID and changed paths, giving a why-did-this-change view alongside almanac_why's blame
    - PR data carries its own freshness — a PR list is stale the moment it is fetched, so every answer states when it was last synced, the same discipline as index staleness
    - "the dedupe checks (ZERO-WASTE-045 gaps, ZERO-WASTE-046 code) gain a third leg: in-flight PRs, so a near-duplicate that is already being built is surfaced before a second agent claims it"
    - remote-cached repos with no worktree still work — this is API metadata, not a filesystem read
    - "no diffs or patches are stored: metadata plus paths only, and the reason is written down"
  notes: |
    SHIPPED 2026-08-09 in almanac 1b295c7 (origin/main), release binaries deployed — almanac_prs live in the MCP server (10 tools).
    
    5 of 6 acceptance criteria met in almanac:
    1. open PRs queryable with changed paths — 'almanac prs <repo> --touching PATH'
       (exact file or directory prefix). Live: 16 PRs synced from chump.
    2. recently-merged PRs queryable with title, gap ID, changed paths; window
       bounded (--merged-window, default 30).
    3. freshness stated FIRST, not as a footnote — synced_at, or a loud
       NEVER-SYNCED warning so an empty list cannot read as 'no work in flight'.
    5. remote-cached repos work — verified on pixi-game (no worktree at all):
       origin-based API read, sync completed.
    6. no diffs or patches; metadata + paths only, reason written into the schema
       comment (the patch is already in git).
    
    AC #4 NOT DONE — it is chump-side (ZERO-WASTE-045/046 gaining an in-flight
    dedupe leg) and this session is working external-to-chump by operator
    instruction. Filed to holler instead: kind=suggestion, project=chump,
    context.repo=repairman29/chump, tier=operator_verified, titled 'Dedupe needs a
    third leg: in-flight PRs (ZERO-WASTE-045/046 + almanac_prs)'. It will bridge
    into a chump gap on the next holler-to-chump --apply.
    
    ALSO NOT DONE and flagged in that same holler row: nothing syncs PRs on a
    cadence yet. The hourly refresh cron is the natural home.
    
    RECEIPT that this prevents real waste: on 2026-08-09 I built #3534 against
    .github/workflows/audit.yml while #3532 was already open against the same
    file. Both merged. That is now one query:
      almanac prs chump --touching .github/workflows/audit.yml
  closed_date: '2026-08-09'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: EFFECTIVE-000

