- id: INFRA-2015
  domain: INFRA
  title: "EFFECTIVE P1: A2A typed urgency levels — broadcast.sh adds --urgency INFO|WARN|CRIT|EMERGENCY routing"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: s
  description: |
    Today (pre-INFRA-2015): broadcast.sh accepts an event-type
    (INTENT|HANDOFF|STUCK|DONE|WARN|ALERT|FEEDBACK) but those describe
    INTENT not URGENCY. Caller might WARN about something low-priority
    or ALERT about something already-resolved — receiver has no way to
    triage.
    
    With urgency: sender declares "this needs response in <window>"
    independently from event-type. Routing layer matches urgency to
    delivery mechanism.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "broadcast.sh adds --urgency flag: INFO (default, file-only — next-session pickup), WARN (file + kind=urgent_broadcast ambient emit — in-loop curators tick within 5min), CRIT (file + ambient + tmux send-keys via INFRA-2014 injector), EMERGENCY (file + ambient + pane-kill + restart with --read-inbox-first)"
    - Outbound message metadata adds urgency field (preserved in inbox/<session>.jsonl); chump-inbox.sh display surfaces urgency in colored output
    - Default urgency for existing callers stays INFO so no behavior change for legacy senders
    - "Smoke test scripts/ci/test-broadcast-urgency-routing.sh: 4 messages at 4 urgency levels, assert each takes the correct route"
    - "Doc update: docs/process/OPUS_MESSAGE_PROTOCOL.md — urgency table + when-to-use guidance ('CRIT = pile-up cluster fired and worker is about to push into it; EMERGENCY = data-loss-imminent like the PR #2582 stale-base force-push class')"
