- id: PRODUCT-193
  domain: PRODUCT
  title: "[almanac] INFRA-3530 (SQL indexing) is open as a capability gap but ROADMAP defers it by design — reconcile"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Confidence: operator_verified -- a human or session directly investigated and confirmed this before filing -- high confidence.
    
    Two sources of truth disagree about whether almanac should index SQL.
    
    Chump gap INFRA-3530 is OPEN, framed as a capability gap: "almanac never indexes SQL — blind to migrations/RLS/schema".
    
    ROADMAP.md (entry dated 2026-08-04, commit 4437452) defers it BY DESIGN with a stated reason: indexing migration files would serve stale schema with confidence, because the fleet's deployed mega-DB has drifted from its migrations. An agent asking about RLS would get authoritative-looking wrong answers. The live-schema path (Supabase MCP) is already doctrine.
    
    So the gap reads as unfinished work, while the roadmap reads as a deliberate decision. Whoever picks up INFRA-3530 next will either re-derive that reasoning or, worse, implement the thing the roadmap argues against.
    
    Fix direction: either close INFRA-3530 as wontfix citing the ROADMAP rationale, or re-scope it to what the roadmap actually leaves open — "index SQL ONLY with a drift warning attached to every hit (this is what migrations SAY, not what the database IS)". The second is a real, buildable gap; the current framing is not.
    
    Note the sibling gap INFRA-3529 (fusion ranking disadvantages doc-only files) IS still a genuine open gap and is unaffected by this.
    
