- id: RESILIENT-157
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: INFRA-705 stall-detector false-positives on legit cargo builds — kills worker cycles mid-clippy (silent != stuck)"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - 1. The INFRA-705 stall-detector in scripts/dispatch/worker.sh, before killing a cycle on 'no output >= CHUMP_STALL_THRESHOLD_S', checks whether a build process (cargo
    - rustc
    - clippy-driver
    - "sccache) is an ACTIVE DESCENDANT of the claude -p PID (process-tree walk via pgrep -P or ps). If an active build is found → the cycle is progressing (compiling), so RESET the stall timer / skip the kill this interval (emit kind=stall_deferred_build with the build pid+cmd for audit). 2. A genuine stall (no output AND no active build descendant) still kills at the threshold (no regression of real-stall detection). 3. Test (scripts/ci/test-stall-detector-build-aware.sh): a fixture where a long no-output window has a live 'cargo'/'sleep'-named descendant → assert NOT killed; same window with NO build descendant → assert killed. 4. Complement (optional, same or follow-up PR): warm the build cache at fleet launch (rebuild chump / shared cargo target) since the 21h-stale binary + 27-crate/446-file workspace make cold clippy 3-5min. 5. Emit/document the new kind=stall_deferred_build in EVENT_REGISTRY.yaml."
  closed_pr: 3150
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    OBSERVED 2026-06-21 23:23: worker 2 cycle on INFRA-900 killed by 'INFRA-705 stall-detector firing (no output for 123s >= 120s)'. But the agent log (/tmp/chump-fleet-*/agent-2-cycle1-INFRA-900.log) proves it was a FALSE POSITIVE: claude was actively working (33 turns, editing src/main.rs), said 'Let me wait for clippy to finish' / 'Clippy is still running', and COMPLETED SUCCESSFULLY (result: success, duration_ms=357937 ~6min). It ran 'cargo clippy --workspace' which on this codebase (27 crates, 446 .rs files, 21h-stale build cache) compiles 3-5+ min streaming NO output to claude stdout → the 120s no-output detector mistook busy-compiling for stuck. ROOT: worker.sh stall-detector (CHUMP_STALL_THRESHOLD_S=120) watches only claude stdout idle time; it has NO build-awareness. Cost: wasted cycle + the gap left incomplete + re-pick churn on every Rust-heavy gap (the fleet's core work). This is the 'sonnet-mid-task-stall' VOA class — but the cause is the MONITOR, not the model.

- id: RESILIENT-158
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: worker pool (run-fleet) has NO durable owner — dies with launching session, nothing revives it"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "1. fleet-autopilot.sh heartbeat tick calls an ensure_worker_pool step: if 'tmux has-session chump-fleet' is false OR it has fewer than CHUMP_FLEET_TARGET_SIZE (default 2) live worker panes, relaunch run-fleet.sh detached with oauth env (CHUMP_AUTH_MODE=oauth) — IDEMPOTENT: kill a stale/empty session first; never double-launch a healthy pool. 2. Durability via tmux: run-fleet creates a detached tmux session (tmux server is process-independent) so the pool survives the heartbeat process exit + the next launchd StartInterval cycle re-ensures it. 3. Emits kind=worker_pool_revived {target, found, action} on relaunch; registered in EVENT_REGISTRY.yaml. 4. Operator off-switch: CHUMP_FLEET_KEEPER_DISABLE=1 makes ensure_worker_pool a no-op (on-demand mode) without code edits. 5. Test (test-fleet-autopilot-worker-pool.sh): ensure_worker_pool is a no-op when a healthy chump-fleet session exists (mock tmux), and invokes the relaunch path when absent (mock run-fleet captures the call). 6. The heartbeat must NOT block on run-fleet (background the relaunch) so the 300s tick stays fast."
  closed_pr: 3151
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    ROOT of the recurring 'fleet keeps dying' (operator flagged 2026-06-21). The durable control plane is healthy: dev.chump.farmer is running (launchctl runs=23358, KeepAlive), fleet-autopilot runs every StartInterval (runs=2803) maintaining curators + base daemons. BUT NOTHING durably owns the run-fleet WORKER POOL (the tmux chump-fleet session of worker.sh panes that actually pick+ship gaps). run-fleet is launched per-session; its fleet-autorestart-daemon lives INSIDE run-fleet's process group so both die together. PROOF: launched fleet 23:14 (workers actively building INFRA-815/INFRA-900 on sonnet), but the launch log shows 'Terminated: 15' (SIGTERM) on run-fleet.sh:768 + fleet-autorestart-daemon.sh:332 when the launching Claude session's process group was torn down → whole chump-fleet session gone by 23:46, 0 ships. Every prior 'revival' this session was a human (me) relaunching = the rescue treadmill the operator wants gone. CURE: extend fleet-autopilot.sh (existing durable launchd heartbeat) to ENSURE the worker pool is up — idempotent: if tmux chump-fleet has < target live worker panes, relaunch run-fleet detached (setsid) with oauth env. No new daemon (extends existing durable machinery). Makes the worker pool session-independent + auto-revived = 'the machine operates' instead of 'I operate the machine'.

- id: RESILIENT-159
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: run-fleet.sh launches LEAK orphaned procs without producing a pool (49 found) — bedrock of 'fleet keeps dying'"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "1. run-fleet.sh installs a trap (EXIT/INT/TERM) that kills its background children — the token-refresher 'sleep 300' loop AND the fleet-autorestart-daemon — so a launcher that exits or fails tmux setup NEVER leaves orphans (PPID 1). 2. The token-refresher + autorestart daemon self-exit when 'tmux has-session chump-fleet' is false (checked each loop iteration) so they die with the pool. 3. Idempotency lock: run-fleet.sh refuses to start (exit 0 + log) if a chump-fleet tmux session already exists OR another run-fleet launcher holds a startup lock (flock/pidfile) — kills the duplicate pile-up that produced 49 orphans. 4. RESILIENT-158 keeper hardening: after _keeper_launch_fleet, verify within ~120s that chump-fleet exists with >= target live panes; else emit worker_pool_launch_failed and do NOT leave a stuck launcher. 5. Test: a launch that fails tmux setup leaves ZERO orphaned sleep-300/daemon procs; a second launch while one is starting no-ops. 6. After merge+deploy+verified-clean, re-enable the keeper (rm ~/.chump/FLEET_KEEPER_OFF), confirm pool comes up AND stays up across one heartbeat with NO orphan growth (pgrep -fc run-fleet.sh stays bounded)."
  closed_pr: 3152
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    2026-06-22 01:16: activating the RESILIENT-158 always-on keeper revealed the bedrock disease. Found 49 orphaned run-fleet.sh processes (PPID 1, ages 2-11h, each looping a 'sleep 300' token-refresher child) with ZERO working pool (no chump-fleet tmux, no claude workers). So EVERY run-fleet launch that doesn't complete tmux setup leaves its background loops (token refresher, autorestart daemon) orphaned + stuck forever; ~49 piled up over ~11h. This is the deeper cause under the whole 'fleet keeps dying' thread — even when launched, run-fleet often orphans instead of creating a pool. The RESILIENT-158 keeper would AMPLIFY it (relaunch every 5min -> +1 orphan); hit the kill-switch (~/.chump/FLEET_KEEPER_OFF) to stop it. ROOT: run-fleet.sh does not tie its background subprocesses to the fleet/launcher lifecycle, and a launch that fails tmux setup exits without reaping them. CURE: (a) trap EXIT/TERM in run-fleet.sh to kill its background children (token refresher 'sleep 300' loop, autorestart daemon) on exit; (b) those daemons should self-exit when the chump-fleet tmux session is gone (poll tmux has-session); (c) idempotency guard: run-fleet refuses to start if a chump-fleet session or another starting launcher exists (kill the duplicate pile-up); (d) the RESILIENT-158 keeper must VERIFY the pool came up (tmux session + live panes within ~120s) else log failure instead of leaving an orphan. Keeper stays OFF until this lands.

- id: RESILIENT-160
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: keeper-launched run-fleet's token refresher dies with the heartbeat process group → oauth not auto-refreshed (~1h relaunch cycle)"
