- id: RESILIENT-211
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: canary rollout for self-deploy — stage to one fleet node before the second (Helsinki + closetjunky)
  status: done
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Context (2026-07-29, operator discussion on environment tiering for ChumpOS): MISSION-012
    self-deploy pushes straight from merged-main to the running binary with no staged
    rollout step. This is the same shape that produced the 13-day-stale-binary incident
    and the class of silent-outage failures already documented (June 22 - July 18 fleet
    dark period; auth-cache + lid-close class outages).
    
    Rejected approach: a full named dev/test/stage/prod environment ceremony. Heavyweight
    for a solo-operator fast-moving project, fights the throughput work already underway,
    and doesn't match how the rest of Chump already works (ephemeral worktree-per-gap
    isolation, not persistent named environments).
    
    Correct-sized approach: Chump already runs a two-node fleet (Helsinki = chump-local +
    broker, closetjunky = claude, per docs/process/ADD_A_FLEET_NODE.md, RESILIENT-191) and
    already has the health checks needed to gate a rollout (bash scripts/dev/mission-scoreboard.sh,
    chump fleet doctor --slo-check). Canary rollout = deploy the new binary to ONE node
    first, run the existing health checks against it, only then roll to the second node.
    No new environment, no new named tier -- just sequencing + a gate using tooling that
    already exists.
    
    Rough shape (not pre-sliced, per two-phase decomposition doctrine):
    (a) identify the current self-deploy trigger path (MISSION-012's daemon/script) and
        where it currently pushes to both nodes simultaneously (or verify if it already
        doesn't -- confirm actual current behavior before assuming)
    (b) add a sequencing step: deploy to node A, wait for a health-check pass
        (mission-scoreboard.sh verdict != STALLED/DRIFTING-on-deploy-lag, fleet doctor
        --slo-check exit 0), then deploy to node B
    (c) on health-check failure at node A, do NOT proceed to node B; surface via ambient
        event (kind=canary_rollout_failed) rather than a silent partial-deploy state
    (d) this is a small sequencing/gating change to existing deploy tooling, not new
        infrastructure -- scope small
  acceptance_criteria:
    - self-deploy rolls out to Helsinki and closetjunky sequentially, not simultaneously
    - a health-check gate (mission-scoreboard.sh or fleet doctor --slo-check) must pass on the first node before the second node receives the new binary
    - a failed health check on the first node blocks rollout to the second and emits an ambient event (kind=canary_rollout_failed) rather than failing silently
    - "test: a simulated unhealthy first-node deploy does not propagate to the second node"
  closed_date: '2026-07-30'
  closed_pr: 3408

- id: RESILIENT-212
  domain: RESILIENT
