- id: RESILIENT-239
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "cargo shared-target I/O tax: 52GB/363k-file deps dir makes warm no-op check 70s at 18% CPU (57s pure stat) — sweep + bound incremental"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "MEASURED BEFORE/AFTER recorded in the gap: warm no-op 'cargo check --workspace' drops from 70s wall to <10s wall (baseline 1:10.02 at 18% CPU, 13s CPU floor)"
    - ~/.cargo/chump-shared-target total drops from 61GB/389,175 files to <15GB; debug/deps under 100k files
    - cargo-sweep installed and a scheduled prune (--time 14 --recursive) wired into the same place the other maintenance crons live; prune is idempotent and safe to run while fleet agents build
    - CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 set for CI/worker build paths (fresh worktrees gain nothing from an 11GB incremental cache); interactive checkout keeps incremental on
    - Re-run the 3+ parallelism case from the existing cross-worktree cargo index mutex contention gap and record whether the stall reproduces after the sweep; if it does, this gap does NOT close and the mutex theory gets its own evidence block
    - "No regression: full workspace check from cold still completes (baseline 12:08) and 'chump --version' still starts in <15ms (baseline 8.8ms)"
  notes: |
    [2026-08-28T17:56:19Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=143, rc=143, cycle_log=0B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: MISSION-010
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: time cargo check --workspace --quiet (warm, immediately after an identical successful run); du -sh + find -type f on ~/.cargo/chump-shared-target/debug
    OUTPUT: warm no-op = 5.91s user + 7.15s system, 18% cpu, 1:10.02 total wall — 13s CPU against 70s wall, so ~57s is neither user nor system compute. debug/deps = 52GB across 362,902 files; debug/incremental = 11GB; debug total = 61GB / 389,175 files. Cold-ish full workspace check for comparison = 12:08.
    THEORY: cargo stats its entire fingerprint + deps tree on every invocation. The shared target-dir (Chump/target symlinks to ~/.cargo/chump-shared-target per .cargo/config.toml target-dir) accumulates artifacts across every worktree, feature-flag combo, and toolchain, and cargo never GCs it. At 363k files the per-invocation stat walk dominates wall-clock, which is why utilization sits at 18% instead of being CPU-bound. Predicts the same root cause behind the existing cross-worktree cargo index mutex contention gap at 3+ fleet parallelism.
    ALT: (a) main.rs monolith blast radius — REFUTED by measurement: real appended-fn edit to main.rs (18,808 LOC) = 12.67s vs identical edit to an 88-LOC leaf module = 13.47s, no blast-radius penalty. (b) genuine cold compile — refuted, the 70s run was warm and immediately repeated. (c) APFS slowness generally — testable by re-measuring after a sweep; if wall drops toward the 13s CPU floor, file count was the cause.

- id: RESILIENT-240
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: [almanac] init hard-codes a Tailscale IP — the tool installs cleanly on exactly one machine on Earth"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: xs
  description: |
    Found 2026-08-07 while scoping what it would take to hand Almanac to a beta tester; the beta is now NO-GO on market grounds but this fix stands on its own as internal resilience. almanac init's --embed-url defaults to a Tailscale-only IP, so the FTUE produces a working install on precisely one machine. Fix is small and surgical: default to localhost:11434 (matching the compiled defaults the rest of the code already uses), keep the stable-host path available via explicit --embed-url and the machine config, env still wins. Do NOT touch scripts/ — clone-and-index-fleet.sh, embed-fleet.sh, summarize-supervisor.sh and eval-gate-cron.sh legitimately hard-code Jeff's hosts and paths; they are fleet ops, not product surface.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "almanac init with no flags defaults its embed endpoint to http://localhost:11434, matching DEFAULT_OLLAMA_URL in embed.rs and summarize.rs"
    - a stable/remote host is still reachable via explicit --embed-url and via the persisted machine config; resolution order env > machine config > default is unchanged
    - no private IP remains as a compiled-in default anywhere under crates/ (scripts/ is out of scope and stays as-is)
    - "the giveaway-discipline comment at registry.rs:40 is no longer contradicted by the CLI it describes"
    - a test or documented manual receipt shows a machine with only local Ollama getting a working fusion path from almanac init alone
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: grep -n -B3 -A12 'Init {' crates/almanac-core/src/bin/almanac.rs  |  OUTPUT: line 93 — #[arg(long, default_value = "http://100.101.188.30:11434")] embed_url: String  (helsinki over Tailscale). Contrast crates/almanac-core/src/embed.rs:15 and summarize.rs:22, both DEFAULT_OLLAMA_URL = http://localhost:11434; and registry.rs:40 whose comment reads 'Deliberately NOT a compiled-in default: source stays infra-free (giveaway discipline)'.  |  THEORY: the struct honors infra-freedom but the CLI arg does not, so 'almanac init' persists a tailnet-only IP into ~/.almanac machine-wide. Any machine not on Jeff's tailnet — a rebuilt laptop, a second machine, a beta tester, future-Jeff — silently degrades every semantic query to keyword, because a configured-but-unreachable host is exactly the 3s-probe degradation path. Confirmed live this session: ~/.almanac/registry.json holds embed_url=http://100.101.188.30:11434.  |  ALT: considered 'bundle the embedding model into the binary' (how codebase-memory-mcp avoids inference entirely) — rejected as the fix for THIS gap: it needs an ONNX/candle runtime, bundled weights, a model license review, and it is a one-way embedding-model swap that invalidates every vector in the fleet. A one-line default change buys the same resilience for this use case. Also considered leaving it and documenting — rejected: a default that only works for its author is a single point of failure, which is the RESILIENT pillar's definition.

