- id: RESILIENT-248
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "pr-rescue is blind to the zero-CI-runs case: teach it to detect and re-fire, with an App token (GITHUB_TOKEN cannot)"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "CONTROL MEASUREMENT, same repo same day: PR #3524 (DOC-089, also doc-only) went created 16:49:41Z -> merged 16:57:13Z = 7m32s with CI firing immediately. Same shape, same required checks, 21x spread. The pipeline is not slow; the trigger failed. Do NOT respond to this by tuning checks"
    - "PR-RESCUE IS BLIND ON TWO INDEPENDENT COUNTS and would never have caught it: scripts/coord/pr-rescue.sh:221 selects '.conclusion == \"failure\"' so a PR with ZERO check_runs has no failures and is skipped; and :209 skips anything younger than PR_RESCUE_STALE_HOURS (default 4) while DOC-085 stalled 2h35m. RESILIENT-006 built it to rebase PRs whose CI went red and has since passed on main — it hunts for RED, and this PR was BLANK"
    - "THE TRAP THAT WOULD MAKE A NAIVE FIX SILENTLY USELESS: pr-rescue.yml runs with GH_TOKEN=secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN, and GitHub does not create workflow runs from events triggered by GITHUB_TOKEN. A close/reopen performed with it would produce EXACTLY the zero-run state it is trying to cure, while logging success. This is worse than not fixing it because the instrument would report healthy. Use a GitHub App token — the pattern is already proven in this repo at .github/workflows/queue-driver.yml:48-56 via actions/create-github-app-token@v3 with QUEUE_DRIVER_APP_ID / QUEUE_DRIVER_APP_PRIVATE_KEY"
    - "DETECT ON THE SPECIFIC SIGNATURE, not on age: an OPEN, non-draft PR with ZERO check_runs on its head SHA more than ~20-30 min after creation. Do not reuse the 4h stale threshold — for a total-CI-absence the whole point is catching it in minutes. Keep it a separate code path from the failure-rebase path so the two thresholds stay independent"
    - "RE-ARM AUTO-MERGE AFTER REOPEN. Close/reopen DROPS auto-merge — verified on #3489, its timeline shows auto_merge_disabled at 00:22:42 and a fresh auto_squash_enabled at 00:23:17 only because it was re-armed by hand. A re-fire that does not re-arm converts a stalled PR into a green PR that sits forever, which is a quieter failure than the one being fixed"
    - "GUARDS ARE MANDATORY, this script closes and reopens real PRs in a loop on a 2h cron: (a) act only on the zero-check_runs signature, (b) cap re-fires per run, (c) idempotent — never re-fire the same head SHA twice, so a PR whose CI is genuinely broken cannot be flapped repeatedly, (d) a dry-run mode that logs intent without touching PRs, exercised in review"
    - "PROVE IT ON A FIXTURE, not on the incident: the bug is not reproducible on demand, so assert the DETECTOR against a synthetic PR-like payload with zero check_runs and confirm it fires, plus one with checks present and confirm it does not. A detector that cannot be shown to fire is the EFFECTIVE-407 failure class again"
  closed_date: '2026-08-09'
  closed_pr: 3525
  outcome_id: MISSION-010
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: gh api repos/repairman29/Chump/issues/3489/timeline + gh run list --branch doc-085-almanac-doctrine | OUTPUT: PR #3489 (DOC-085) created 2026-08-06T21:47:37Z, auto_squash_enabled 21:52:46, then NOTHING until closed 00:22:42 and reopened 00:22:44 — at which point 6 workflow runs were created at 00:22:44-46 and it merged 00:31:22. The FIRST run on that branch is 2h35m after open. End-to-end 2h44m of which ~9m was pipeline | THEORY: the pull_request 'opened' webhook did not create workflow runs. Not a draft (no draft filters exist in ci.yml or audit.yml), not a bot author (repairman29), not a base-branch filter (base was main). Close/reopen re-fired it deterministically | ALT: considered and rejected — (a) runs existed but were cancelled: no, cancelled runs still appear in gh run list and there are zero rows before 00:22; (b) GITHUB_TOKEN authorship suppression: does not apply, a human account opened it; (c) queued-not-started: no, queued runs appear as status=queued

- id: RESILIENT-249
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "pr-rescue.sh exits 2 from a git worktree before processing any PR, and reports RESCUED after a rebase that printed 'fatal: invalid upstream'"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  acceptance_criteria:
    - TWO SEPARATE PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS found while shipping RESILIENT-248. Neither was introduced by it; both were A/B'd against unmodified origin/main
    - "DEFECT 1 — worktree fragility. Running 'bash scripts/coord/pr-rescue.sh' from a git worktree (/tmp/chump-<id>) exits 2 immediately after logging 'main HEAD: <sha>', before any PR is processed. Trace ends at cache_lookup_pr for the first PR. From the main checkout the same script exits 0 and processes all three open PRs. PROOF IT IS PRE-EXISTING: 'git show origin/main:scripts/coord/pr-rescue.sh' written into the worktree and run there reproduces exit 2 identically. Note CHUMP_GITHUB_CACHE_RUST was unset, so this is the BASH cache path at scripts/coord/lib/github_cache.sh:237, not the Rust shim at :46"
    - "WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS: every chump claim produces a worktree, so any session that tries to exercise or debug pr-rescue from its own claim cannot run it at all. That is very likely part of why the zero-CI-runs blind spot (RESILIENT-248) survived so long — the instrument is hard to run anywhere except the one checkout the fleet is actively using"
    - "DEFECT 2 — false RESCUED. In the same main-checkout run, PR #3499 logged 'rescue candidate — rebasing onto main', then 'fatal: invalid upstream origin/main', and then 'PR #3499: rebased + re-armed. RESCUED.' with the final tally 'rescued=1 skipped=2 failed=0'. The rebase FAILED and the script counted it as a success. Its own summary line is therefore unreliable, which is the 'signal is not outcome' rule broken inside the tool meant to enforce it"
    - The auto-merge re-arm in that same run was a no-op ('already armed'), so the observable harm was limited to the false count — but a rescue loop that reports success on a failed rebase will mask real stuck PRs indefinitely
    - "FIX BOTH: make the rebase failure propagate to the RESCUED/FAILED tally rather than being swallowed, and make the script runnable from a worktree (or fail loudly with a clear message naming the reason instead of a bare exit 2)"
    - VERIFY by running from a worktree AND from the main checkout, and by forcing a rebase failure and asserting the tally reports failed=1
  notes: |
