- id: RESILIENT-252
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: docker publishes past ufw — the next container on helsinki re-exposes the box silently"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Found while hardening helsinki 2026-08-08. Docker inserts its own iptables rules ahead of ufw's chains, so a container published to 0.0.0.0 is reachable from the internet EVEN WITH ufw enabled and default-deny-incoming. The SearXNG container added the same day is safe only because it was bound explicitly to 100.101.188.30:8888 rather than published broadly — that was a deliberate choice, not a property of the setup, and nothing enforces it for the next container. The trap is that ufw status will read 'active, deny (incoming)' while a published port is wide open, which is precisely the shape of dashboard-lies the fleet has a rule about. Standard mitigations: set iptables=false in /etc/docker/daemon.json and manage rules explicitly, or install the ufw-docker helper and use its rule syntax, or make an operator rule that every container binds a specific interface. Pick one and write it where the next person will look.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - a container published to 0.0.0.0 on helsinki is NOT reachable from the public internet — demonstrated by actually publishing a throwaway container and probing it from outside, then removing it
    - the chosen mitigation is documented where a future container gets run (helsinki runbook or chump docs), not only in this gap
    - the SearXNG container is confirmed to still answer over Tailscale after whatever mitigation lands
    - "ufw status and reality agree: if a port is open, the firewall says so"
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T08:32:42Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6004B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'

- id: RESILIENT-253
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: the EU runner executes workflows as ROOT on the box holding the fleet's bus, embedder and secrets — chump must not go public until this changes"
  status: ready_to_ship
  priority: P1
  effort: s
  description: |
    Two deliverables, and the GATE is the urgent one. (1) GATE: RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md gains an item the release-auditor actually evaluates — no repo may go public while a self-hosted runner registered to it executes as root, or while a runner shares a host with fleet credentials. This is the piece that must exist before anyone flips chump's visibility, because the giveaway track means that decision could arrive on any given day. (2) FIX: de-privilege the lane. Options, cheapest first — run the runner as a dedicated non-root user (GitHub's own recommendation); make it --ephemeral so each job gets a fresh registration; isolate jobs in containers; or move the runner off the host that carries NATS credentials, the embedder and SearXNG entirely. Whichever is chosen, verify the EU CI lane still passes afterward: INFRA-1534/1535/1537 built this lane on purpose and a silent break looks like a healthy-but-quiet organ. Related but distinct: INFRA-1539 guarded sudo/apt steps for compatibility, not for privilege.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md carries a runner-privilege item, and the release-auditor agent evaluates it — a GO cannot be issued for a repo whose self-hosted runner runs as root or shares a host with fleet credentials
    - "the runner no longer executes as root: it runs as a dedicated unprivileged user, or ephemerally, or isolated per job, with the choice and its reasoning written down"
    - /etc/nats/nats.conf and any other fleet credential on that host is unreadable by the runner's user, verified by attempting to read it as that user
    - the EU CI lane still passes after the change — proven by a real workflow run on that runner, not by inspection
    - if the chosen fix is moving the runner off helsinki, the fleet's EU lane is confirmed still routed and green (INFRA-1537 built that routing)
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: ssh helsinki 'ps -o user=,args= -p 1852983; systemctl list-units | grep runner; cat /root/actions-runner/.runner' ; gh repo view repairman29/chump --json visibility  |  OUTPUT: actions.runner.repairman29-chump.chumpd-eu-runner.service, active, running /root/actions-runner/bin/Runner.Listener as user ROOT, scoped to repairman29/chump. Same host also runs: NATS (the fleet event bus, credentialed in /etc/nats/nats.conf), Ollama (almanac's embedder, now tailnet-bound), /root/rerank_server.py, and the SearXNG container. Box is a Hetzner VPS with a public IP (204.168.229.237, IPv6 2a01:4f9:...). chump visibility today: PRIVATE (verified).  |  THEORY: a self-hosted runner executes whatever a workflow instructs, with the runner's privileges. At root on this host that means any workflow — or any dependency a workflow pulls — can read /etc/nats/nats.conf, reach the embedder, install persistence, or take the box. That is acceptable ONLY because chump is private, so only trusted collaborators can add workflow content. The moment chump's visibility flips, a stranger's pull_request becomes root on the machine that runs the fleet's nervous system. GitHub's own documentation warns against self-hosted runners on public repositories for exactly this reason. Shop rule 7 says repos stay private until security-reviewed; nothing currently connects that review to this runner, so the review could pass while the runner is still root.  |  ALT: considered simply de-privileging the runner now — reasonable and preferred, but it is a live CI lane for the fleet's EU work (INFRA-1534/1535/1537 built it deliberately) and yanking privileges blind could red-trunk the fleet, so the gate matters more urgently than the fix. Considered treating this as covered by the existing INFRA-1534..1542 runner family — rejected: those cover autoscaling, path routing, and guarding sudo/apt steps for compatibility; none addresses privilege level or ties the runner to a visibility decision.

