    COMMAND: grep -E '^## Layer' docs/process/FLEET_SLOS.md ; grep -icE 'age|stale|unclaimed|parked' docs/process/FLEET_SLOS.md ; sqlite3 .chump/state.db 'SELECT priority, COUNT(*) FROM gaps WHERE status=open GROUP BY priority'  |  OUTPUT: four SLO layers exist — Fleet Stability, Fleet Productivity, Operator Experience, Pipeline Health — each with targets, measurement sources and an escalation column, enforced by 'chump health --slo-check' which exits non-zero on breach. NOT ONE measures the age of committed work: 2 keyword matches for age/stale/unclaimed/parked, both incidental. Registry: 237 open gaps, of which 153 are P2 (65%) with no promotion path.  |  THEORY: every existing SLO measures FLOW (ship-time P50, waste percentage, restart success, silent-agent events) or CAPACITY (P0 budget). None measures whether a PROMISE is rotting. Four instances found in one session on 2026-08-08, none caught by any SLO: the curator dead 12 days with no alert (RESILIENT-246); MOP-BUCKET at 0% with both children parked below the picker threshold; the factory matrix's three missing chairs parked since 2026-08-05; and 153 P2 gaps where P2 functionally means never rather than later. The fleet ships 16-28 PRs a day and cannot tell you that a load-bearing organ stopped or that a stated campaign has not moved in a fortnight — which is the actual blocker on scaling the fleet, because more workers on an unmeasured backlog just rots more work in parallel.  |  ALT: considered adding gap-level SLA fields (due dates per gap) — rejected as too granular and it invites theatre: every gap acquires a fake deadline. The honest unit is the CLASS: an outcome with children showing no movement, an organ that stopped acting, a priority tier with no exit. Considered folding this into META-328 (the coherence advisor) — rejected: 328 SYNTHESISES mechanical checks and this gap BUILDS one of them; 328 should consume it, not contain it.

- id: RESILIENT-255
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "RESILIENT: 71 of 108 almanac sources are frozen — the untether sweep removed worktrees without backfilling origin_url"
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  description: |
    Backfill origin_url for the 71 orphaned entries so Phase F's probe-and-materialise path actually engages, then make the failure mode impossible to miss again. Derivation: slug -> https://github.com/repairman29/<slug>, VERIFIED per repo with gh before writing (several slugs differ in case or hyphenation from the GitHub name, and today's session already proved a case-sensitive assumption can lose four repos). Any slug that does not resolve is reported, not guessed. Second half, which matters as much: 'missing' must stop being the answer for three different situations — genuinely deleted, un-migrated, and origin-unreachable are distinct states and only one of them is the operator's problem. This is the same honesty rule the retrieval side already follows with fallback_reason.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - origin_url is populated for every orphaned entry whose GitHub repo resolves, verified with gh rather than string-derived, and any slug that does NOT resolve is listed in the run output rather than silently skipped
    - after backfill, 'almanac refresh' re-indexes those repos via the probe path — proven on at least one repo whose origin sha has moved since its indexed_commit
    - refresh distinguishes deleted / un-migrated / origin-unreachable instead of reporting all three as 'missing'
    - almanac repos (or coverage) reports refreshability per source, so a frozen index is visible without running a categorisation script by hand
    - search_fleet or status surfaces that a served index is frozen — today all 71 answer queries with no staleness signal at all, which is the confident-wrong-answer shape
    - "a regression test covers an entry with no worktree and no origin_url: it must report un-migrated, never 'fresh' and never 'missing'"
  notes: |
    SHIPPED 2026-08-09 in almanac 4ccc6b9 + cdf45e5 (repairman29/almanac origin/main). No chump PR — the fix lives in almanac and landed by direct commit.
    
    All six acceptance criteria met:
    1. origin_url backfilled for all 71 orphaned entries, verified against the live
       GitHub repo list via gh (one call, matched case-insensitively) rather than
       string-derived. Three slugs differ from their GitHub name and would each have
       been written as a 404: jarvis->JARVIS, jarvis-premium->JARVIS-Premium,
       mythseeker->MythSeeker. Every slug resolved; an unresolved one would have been
       listed, not skipped. 25 of 71 are archived — still clonable, still refreshable.
    2. refresh re-indexes via the probe path — 'almanac refresh pixi-game' now reports
       'fresh (probe only, no worktree)' off a live ls-remote, impossible before.
    3. 'missing' split into un-migrated / deleted; origin-unreachable already existed.
       Both new branches verified live against a synthetic ALMANAC_HOME.
    4. 'almanac repos' prints refreshability per source (71 remote, 39 worktree,
       0 frozen) and warns on any FROZEN count.
    5. search_fleet + MCP carry the frozen signal — on the HIT, not as a droppable
       response note; absent from JSON when false so existing callers are unaffected.
    6. 6 regression tests: 4 on classify_absent (un-migrated vs deleted, never
       'fresh', never re-collapsed), 2 on the frozen serialization contract.
       DEPTH.md updated in the same commits, naming what they do NOT cover.
    
    CORRECTION to this gap's framing: 0 of the 71 had actually drifted. All origins
    were reachable and every indexed_commit still matched HEAD, so almanac was never
    serving stale CONTENT. The exposure was latent — un-refreshable, not wrong. The
    title said 'frozen', which was right; it implied 'stale', which was not.
    
    NOTE: this work was completed once, destroyed by a sibling session's
    'git reset --hard' on the shared checkout (RESILIENT-256), and rebuilt in a
    worktree with an immediate commit.
  closed_date: '2026-08-09'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"
  outcome_id: RESILIENT-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: python3 categorise ~/.almanac/registry.json by (origin_url present / worktree present / .git present) ; almanac refresh biomeweavers ; sqlite3 ~/.almanac/indexes/biomeweavers.db  |  OUTPUT: 108 registered sources — 34 local-git, 3 local-nongit, 0 remote-tracked, and 71 ORPHAN (no worktree AND no origin_url). 'almanac refresh biomeweavers' prints 'missing'. Its index is NOT missing: biomeweavers.db holds 11 files, indexed_at 2026-08-07, and search_fleet skips only on !db.exists(), so all 71 frozen indexes are still searched and served today.  |  THEORY: Phase F shipped the mechanism correctly — RepoEntry gained origin_url, refresh_entry dispatches to refresh_remote_entry when it is set, and remote_head_sha/materialize_shallow implement probe-then-materialise. The untether sweep then reclaimed 9.4GB by deleting cache worktrees. The two halves were never joined: no migration wrote origin_url for the 71 existing entries, so refresh_entry falls through to the !root.exists() branch and returns 'missing' forever. 66 percent of the fleet index is frozen at whatever commit it last saw, still answering queries with no staleness signal, and the word 'missing' actively misleads — it reads as 'gone' when it means 'un-migrated'.  |  ALT: considered re-cloning all 71 to restore worktrees — rejected, that undoes the 9.4GB reclaim the sweep was built for. Considered dropping the 71 from the registry — rejected: their indexes are real and answering; deleting them loses fleet coverage and violates archive-only. The fix is a backfill, and the URL is derivable: these are repairman29 repos and the slug maps to the GitHub name, verifiable with gh repo view before writing.

- id: RESILIENT-256
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: an agent ran git reset --hard on a SHARED checkout and destroyed ~574 lines of another session's uncommitted work — permanently
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - destructive-git guard refuses reset --hard / checkout -- . / clean -fd against a dirty tree and NAMES every file it would destroy, wired as a PreToolUse hook agents actually invoke (chump git-guard, repo-local .claude/settings.json + fleet-wide installer)
    - agents get their own worktree; the primary checkout is read-mostly, enforced by chump git-guard --hook-write refusing Edit/Write into a primary that live linked worktrees share, plus one-command scripts/dev/own-worktree.sh
    - "uncommitted-work watchdog: WIP idle >3h in any checkout is surfaced AND written to the object store via chump wip-snapshot (refs/wip/, working tree untouched); dev.chump.wip-watchdog launchd, graded as 'wip' by the reaper heartbeat watchdog"
    - regression test proves the destructive command is REFUSED against a dirty tree and PERMITTED against a clean one (scripts/ci/test-git-destructive-guard.sh, wired into audit.yml shard 4)
    - the lost coverage trust-signal was rebuilt separately in almanac 279b180 and is NOT still missing
  notes: |
    Root cause was three stacked failures, and the reset is the LEAST interesting: (1) work sat UNCOMMITTED for 13h in a shared checkout — the one loss class git cannot recover; (2) the agent worked in the PRIMARY checkout other sessions share instead of its own worktree; (3) reset --hard ran against a dirty tree with no check. Git has NO pre-reset hook, so a hook-based fix inside git is impossible; the design weights the object-store snapshot (criterion 3) as load-bearing and the refusal (criterion 1) as the seatbelt. Recovery of the original bytes was attempted and IS impossible (git fsck --lost-found + cat-file over every dangling commit/tree/blob found nothing). NOTE: the destroyed coverage trust-signal for MCP responses was REBUILT SEPARATELY in almanac commit 279b180 — it is not still missing.
  closed_pr: 3549
  outcome_id: CREDIBLE-000
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: cd ~/Projects/almanac && git reflog -6
    OUTPUT:
      869d896 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main
      869d896 HEAD@{1}: checkout: moving from main to main
      869d896 HEAD@{2}: branch: Reset to origin/main
    
    COMMAND: git fsck --lost-found, then cat-file every dangling commit/tree for
      crates/almanac-core/src/coverage.rs containing 'measure_from_store'
    OUTPUT: NOT FOUND in any dangling object. 16 loose objects exist from the
      2026-08-08 20:00-22:00 authoring window; none carry the content. Unstaged
      work never entered the object store, so there is nothing to recover.
    
    WHAT WAS LOST: ~574 insertions across 7 files in the almanac PRIMARY checkout
    (coverage.rs +51, embed.rs +98, fusion.rs +29, almanac-mcp/src/main.rs +224,
    almanac-mcp/tests/roundtrip.rs +93, scripts/embed-fleet.sh +115, README.md +23):
    a coverage trust-signal so an MCP search response carries its own reliability
    marker inline (measure_from_store — measure a repo from an already-open Store,
    no second DB open on the hot path). Authored 2026-08-08 20:40-21:10, still
    uncommitted 13h later. A second session's in-flight work (RESILIENT-255) died
    in the same reset.
    
    THEORY: three failures stacked; the reset is the least interesting one.
      1. Work sat UNCOMMITTED for 13 hours in a shared checkout. Unstaged changes
         are the one class git cannot recover — no reflog, no fsck, no stash. It
         was one cleanup away from gone the whole time, reset or not.
      2. Agents work directly in the PRIMARY checkout other sessions share, rather
         than in their own worktree. almanac already has .claude/worktrees/.
      3. reset --hard ran against a dirty tree with no check; git has no pre-reset
         hook, so nothing could warn.
    The lost work also left the suite RED (added a test, never updated DEPTH.md's
    count), so it violated shop rule #8 and was never shippable as it sat.
    
    ALT rejected: 'tell agents to be careful'. This is the SECOND multi-session git
    incident — the first is already in auto-memory as 'ship via git fetch . branch:main
    when the primary checkout belongs to a sibling session'. A rule that already
    existed and was already violated is not a fix. ALT rejected: make reset itself
    safe — git offers no pre-reset hook. The durable fix is that work reaches the
    object store EARLY, so recovery stays possible even when the destructive command
    is legitimate.

