- id: RESILIENT-275
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "a --features build in ANY worktree deletes the fleet's chump binary mid-build: shared target dir has no isolation"
  status: done
  priority: P0
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "BLAST RADIUS, and why this is P0: the fleet runs ~145 workers that shell out to chump. During any ad-hoc release build they get \"No such file or directory\" — not a stale binary, an ABSENT one. Failures land wherever chump happened to be invoked, look unrelated to each other, and vanish when the build finishes, so they read as flakes. This session already produced one"
    - "SECOND FAILURE MODE, observed the same evening and arguably worse because it is SILENT: a --features discord build put a discord-enabled binary at the shared path, and a later default build REPLACED it minutes later. Anything depending on an opted-in feature loses it with no error and no log line. The RESILIENT-266 daemon hit exactly this — the installer refused to install because the binary it had just verified no longer had --discord"
    - THE FIX IS NOT TO REVERT ZERO-WASTE-029. Per-repo target dirs cost 54GB and caused a disk-100% incident; that decision was right. The gap is that a SHARED WRITE TARGET needs either isolation or a publish step
    - "OPTION A, isolation: ad-hoc and worktree builds get their own CARGO_TARGET_DIR (the pattern the git hooks already use per INFRA-1374), leaving the shared dir for one designated builder. Costs disk, which is what ZERO-WASTE-029 was avoiding — measure before choosing"
    - "OPTION B, publish step, and the one already proven tonight: builds write wherever they like, and consumers read a STABLE published path. RESILIENT-266 does this — the installer copies to ~/.chump/bin/chump-discord so the fleet cannot clobber the daemon. Generalise it: workers execute a published binary, never a build output"
    - WHICHEVER IS CHOSEN, MAKE THE ABSENCE WINDOW IMPOSSIBLE. Even with a shared dir, publishing should be atomic — build to a temp name, then rename into place. A rename is atomic on the same filesystem, so consumers see the old binary or the new one, never nothing
    - "VERIFY BY REPRODUCTION, not by inspection: start a release build in a worktree and, while it runs, invoke chump from the main checkout in a loop. Today that produces \"No such file or directory\". After the fix it must produce a working binary for every invocation"
    - WHILE THIS IS OPEN, warn in the installer and in CLAUDE.md that a --features build in a worktree takes the fleet binary down for the duration
  closed_pr: 3553
  outcome_id: CHUMPOS
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: cargo build --release --features discord --bin chump, run from the claim worktree /tmp/chump-resilient-266, then ls the fleet binary during the build | OUTPUT: ls: /Users/jeffadkins/.cargo/chump-shared-target/release/chump: No such file or directory — while the build was still running (2 cargo procs live). ~/Projects/Chump/target is a SYMLINK to that shared dir (verified: same inode 556232956), so the fleet path ~/Projects/Chump/target/release/chump was simultaneously dead. An unrelated command in this session failed with "no such file or directory: ./target/release/chump" as a direct result | THEORY: ZERO-WASTE-029 points ALL local cargo builds at ~/.cargo/chump-shared-target via .cargo/config.toml [build] target-dir, to stop per-repo target/ dirs eating disk (the 54GB 2026-07-26 incident). It achieved that, but it also removed ALL isolation: any build in any of ~145 worker worktrees replaces — and mid-build, DELETES — the single binary every other worker executes | ALT: rejected "only feature builds do this" — a plain cargo build relinks the same path. The feature build makes it worse and longer (14m27s vs a warm rebuild) but the hazard is structural, not feature-specific. Also rejected "workers use their own target dirs" — the pre-commit/pre-push hooks set INFRA-1374 isolated dirs, but ad-hoc builds (curators, interactive sessions, this one) explicitly do NOT, which is the exact case config.toml was written to capture

- id: RESILIENT-276
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: "no timeout on provider LLM calls: one slot hung 4m45s, failover never fired, and the phone showed an infinite typing indicator"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "THREE DEFECTS THAT COMPOUND, and the combination is what makes this P1 rather than a slow-model annoyance: (1) no per-call timeout, so a hung slot stalls indefinitely; (2) no slot/model identity in the logs, so the offending slot cannot be named (CREDIBLE-266); (3) the Discord typing indicator persists with no progress and no cap, so from the phone a hung provider and hard thinking look IDENTICAL. The operator had no way to know whether to wait or give up"
    - FIX 1 — BOUND THE CALL AND FAIL OVER. Put a timeout on the agent-loop provider call and treat expiry as a slot failure, so the cascade advances to the next slot. The 15-slot ladder exists precisely for this and never got a chance to fire. Make the value configurable (CHUMP_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_S) with a sane default; 4m45s must not be reachable by accident
    - "FIX 2 — RECORD THE TIMEOUT AS A FAILURE SIGNATURE, not just a retry. A slot that times out repeatedly is a faucet closing, and that is exactly the no-probing detection path EFFECTIVE-366 and RESILIENT-270 depend on: learn it from real traffic instead of spending quota on synthetic pings. Blocked on CREDIBLE-266 for the identity — without it a timeout event cannot name which slot to demote"
    - FIX 3 — THE PHONE MUST NEVER SHOW AN INFINITE ELLIPSIS. This is now a user-facing surface (RESILIENT-266 shipped it). Either send a progress edit after N seconds, or hard-cap the turn and reply with what is known plus why it stopped. Silence that is indistinguishable from a hang is the same failure class as the operator-recall handler looking healthy while dead
    - "VERIFY BY REPRODUCTION, not inspection: point a slot at an endpoint that accepts the connection and never responds, send a DM, and assert three things — the call is cut at the configured timeout, the cascade advances to the next slot and answers, and the ambient event names the timed-out slot"
    - "BASELINE FOR COMPARISON, measured on the same gateway the same evening: two tool-free turns returned in provider_ms 309 and 503 (total 321 and 521), and this one took 289417. Sub-second is the norm; four minutes is the anomaly"
  depends_on: [CREDIBLE-266]
  notes: |
    Decomposed into 6 slices: RESILIENT-452, RESILIENT-453, RESILIENT-454, RESILIENT-455, RESILIENT-456, RESILIENT-457
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: CHUMPOS
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: operator DM to the live Discord gateway asking what the fleet is up to, 2026-08-09T20:24:12Z, then read ~/.chump/logs/discord-gateway.err | OUTPUT: INFRA-185 phase timings request_id=d2c55a47-e749-4cd0-9923-67724e201513 compaction_ms=0 provider_ms=289417 tools_ms=53 other_ms=10 rounds=3 total_ms=289480. The stall is visible between two consecutive log lines: iter=2 called the LLM at 20:24:15.890999Z and the next event is at 20:29:01.378160Z — 4m45s inside ONE provider call. Tools took 53ms total, so the fleet lookup itself was fast | THEORY: there is no bounded timeout on the agent loop LLM call, so a slow or hung slot stalls the whole turn instead of failing over. Searched src/agent_loop/ for timeout and Duration::from_secs and found NONE; other subsystems do set them (src/memory_tool.rs:67/95/541 use 10s, src/telegram.rs:78 sets one). No CHUMP_*TIMEOUT* env var is set either | ALT: rejected the model was simply thinking — rounds=3 with tools_ms=53 means the work was trivial; and two earlier turns the same evening on the same gateway returned in provider_ms 309 and 503. Also rejected network-wide failure — the turn eventually COMPLETED, so the connection was alive the whole time

- id: RESILIENT-277
  domain: RESILIENT
  title: duplicate approval cards make the approval surface UNANSWERABLE — taps resolve orphaned request_ids and the turn stays hung
  status: done
  priority: P1
  effort: m
  acceptance_criteria:
    - "ESCALATED 2026-08-09 AFTER A LIVE TAP: the operator tapped a button and got \"another button then hung\". This is not spam, it is an UNUSABLE approval surface — the feature RESILIENT-265 depends on does not work when a human actually tries to use it"
    - "ROOT CAUSE: the retry loop mints a NEW request_id per attempt, so three cards carry three DIFFERENT ids. The loop abandons the earlier receivers and waits on the latest, so tapping any older card calls resolve_approval on an id with NO receiver — approval_resolver::resolve_approval does a guard.remove(request_id) and silently drops an unknown id. The tap is accepted by Discord, does nothing, and the turn stays blocked. From the phone that is indistinguishable from a broken button"
    - "THE custom_ids ARE CORRECT — verified src/discord.rs:671/673 mint chump_approve and chump_deny with the rid, and interaction_create at :1210 parses exactly those prefixes. Do NOT go hunting a prefix mismatch; the ids are fine, the TARGETS are stale"
    - "FIX 1, DEDUPE IN-FLIGHT: before minting a request_id, check approval_resolver::is_pending for the same tool and normalized args, and JOIN the existing request rather than creating a second. One question, one card, one live receiver"
    - "FIX 2, AWAIT INSTEAD OF SPINNING: the loop must await the oneshot with CHUMP_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_SECS, not re-enter the model. Log evidence: iter=3 through 8 alternating LLM calls with EndTurn synthetic tools, model_calls 2 to 7 in about 13 seconds. Each spin costs a provider call AND creates the orphan above"
    - FIX 3, SUPERSEDE STALE CARDS — this is what makes the surface honest. When a request is abandoned or resolved, EDIT its message to say superseded and disable its buttons. A card that looks tappable but resolves nothing is worse than no card at all
    - FIX 4, MAKE TAPS OBSERVABLE. interaction_create logs NOTHING on success and silently returns on an unmatched custom_id, so a tap that arrived and a tap that never came look identical in the log. That is why this needed a live operator report instead of showing up in telemetry. Log the request_id, the decision, and whether a receiver was actually found — resolve_approval should distinguish HIT from ORPHAN
    - "VERIFY BY REPRODUCTION: trigger a gated tool, do not tap, assert exactly ONE card appears and model_calls does not climb while waiting. Then tap Deny and assert the log records a receiver HIT and the turn ends with the denial honoured. Then tap a superseded card and assert it is inert and says so"
    - "RELATED: RESILIENT-276 (no provider timeout) is the same root shape — the agent loop has no bounded wait for anything slow or human, so it spins instead of waiting. Fix them together"
    - "RECONCILE (2026-08-20): already shipped via PR #4029 (f90d5744, \"dedupe\nin-flight approvals, supersede stale cards, log taps\") — an ancestor of\ncurrent main (in fact HEAD at claim time) — but state.db has no row for\nthis gap (`chump gap show RESILIENT-277` reports not found), so this\nYAML never flipped to done. Same stale-record class as the CREDIBLE-269\nreconcile (#4028).\n\nVerified all 4 fixes live in src/, independent of the PR description:\n- FIX 1 (dedupe): src/approval_resolver.rs `request_approval_for`\n  joins an in-flight (tool, normalized-args) request via a `watch`\n  channel instead of minting a new id (AC4).\n- FIX 2 (await, not spin): src/task_executor.rs:341 awaits the\n  receiver with `approval_timeout_secs()` under a\n  \"RESILIENT-277 FIX 2 (already in place)\" comment (AC5).\n- FIX 3 (supersede stale cards) + FIX 4 (observable taps):\n  src/discord.rs tracks each card's message id, edits any still-\n  pending card to \"superseded\" with buttons disabled when the turn\n  ends, and `resolve_approval` returns `ResolveOutcome::Hit`/`Orphan`\n  so every tap is logged as a receiver hit or an orphan (AC6, AC7).\n- approval_resolver.rs carries unit tests for join-on-same-args,\n  no-join-on-different-args, joined-receiver-observes-resolution,\n  and abandon-removes-pending-state — covering the dedupe/orphan\n  behavior AC8 asks to verify by reproduction.\n"
  closed_pr: 4029
  outcome_id: CHUMPOS
  evidence: |
    COMMAND: operator DM to the live Discord gateway 2026-08-09T20:31Z, then read ~/.chump/logs/discord-gateway.err | OUTPUT: Discord shows THREE identical cards, "Approve tool? run_cli (risk: low). no high-risk pattern" each with Allow once / Deny, and the bot still typing. The log shows a tight retry loop: iter=3 EndTurn, iter=4 EndTurn, iter=5 EndTurn, iter=6, iter=7, iter=8, with model_calls climbing 2 to 7 between 20:32:07 and 20:32:32 — roughly 13 seconds — and a native tool batch with pending_count=4 | THEORY: the tool needs approval, request_approval() blocks on a tap that has not come, the model retries, and each retry mints a NEW approval request instead of joining the one already pending. There is no dedupe on (tool, args) for in-flight approvals | ALT: rejected the model is confused by the question — it answered the previous DM correctly at 2:29PM ("task queue is empty, brain shows beast-mode and chump-chassis"). Rejected a Discord duplicate-delivery problem — the log shows genuinely distinct iterations and rising model_calls, so these are three real requests, not one message delivered three times

