- id: ZERO-WASTE-044
  domain: ZERO-WASTE
  title: "ZERO-WASTE: [almanac] dead-symbol CANDIDATE report — zero-importer symbols minus entry points, honest about its false-positive floor"
  status: blocked
  priority: P2
  effort: s
  description: |
    Borrowed idea (2026-08-07) from DeusData/codebase-memory-mcp v0.9.0 (MIT): 'dead code detection — finds functions with zero callers, excluding entry points', ~150ms via full graph scan with degree filtering. Almanac already stores the import graph and computes in-degree for architecture hub-ranking, so the report is mostly assembly. Deliberately filed P2, not P1: the value is real but bounded, and the honesty burden is high. Almanac resolves only ~20-40 percent of import edges on large repos (the number is reported by every impact/architecture call), and it walks FILE-level import edges, not call graphs — so 'zero importers' is much weaker evidence of deadness than their type-inferred call graph gives them. This ships as a CANDIDATE list or it does not ship: an over-confident dead-code report that gets acted on is how real code gets deleted. Pairs with ZERO-WASTE-036 (the wiring-sweep curator) as a detection input; it is NOT that gap — this is one report, not an act-on-detection loop.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - reports symbols/files with zero resolved importers, excluding entry points (bins, lib roots, tests, examples, and anything reachable from a manifest entrypoint)
    - every line of output is labelled a CANDIDATE and carries the repo's actual edge-resolution rate, so a reader cannot mistake a floor for a finding
    - "the report states its two structural limits in its own output: file-level import edges are not a call graph, and unresolved edges are invisible"
    - never deletes, never rewrites, never auto-files — the archive-only rule holds; output is a list a human or a curator gap acts on
    - at least one test with a fixture where a symbol is imported only via an UNRESOLVED edge, proving that symbol is NOT reported as dead (the false-positive case that matters)
    - attribution note for the borrowed idea (DeusData/codebase-memory-mcp, MIT) recorded at borrow time
  notes: |
    [2026-08-29T08:12:14Z] INFRA-3832 auto-block: 3 consecutive non-ship cycles (last kind=rc=1, rc=1, cycle_log=6005B). Worker kept re-picking + looping; blocked to leave the pick pool. Un-block after fixing the spec / decomposing.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  skills_required: "external_repo:repairman29/almanac"

- id: ZERO-WASTE-045
  domain: ZERO-WASTE
  title: "ZERO-WASTE: dedupe gaps at reserve time — almanac already indexes all 3,489 gap YAMLs, nothing queries them"
  status: open
  priority: P2
  effort: m
  description: |
    DOC-082 doctrine says check for an existing gap before filing; it is a manual step, and it failed twice in one day producing superseded gaps within minutes. Measured 2026-08-07: almanac's chump index already holds 3,489 rows under docs/gaps/, so the whole registry is keyword- and (post-INFRA-3529) heading-searchable RIGHT NOW with zero new indexing work. The build is a pre-flight query inside 'chump gap reserve': search the proposed title against docs/gaps/ plus recent merged PR subjects, print near-matches with their IDs and status, and require an explicit confirm (or --no-dedupe-check) before minting an ID. Cheap, and it attacks a documented recurring waste.
  acceptance_criteria:
    - chump gap reserve runs a title similarity check against the indexed docs/gaps/ corpus BEFORE minting an ID and prints any near-matches with gap ID, status, and citation
    - "a near-match does not hard-block: the operator or agent can proceed with an explicit flag, and that choice is recorded in the gap notes so a later audit can see the dupe was considered, not missed"
    - closed and shipped gaps are included in the search — the DOC-082 failure mode was re-filing something already SHIPPED, not something open
    - "the check degrades safely: if almanac is unreachable or the index is stale, reserve still works and says loudly that the dedupe check did not run (never a silent skip — cf CREDIBLE-214)"
    - a test proves that reserving a title near-identical to an existing gap surfaces that gap, and fails without the change
  notes: |
    THREE live receipts from the 2026-08-07 session, all by the agent that filed this gap, all AFTER filing it: (1) CREDIBLE-217 filed as a CONFIG-organ cadence sweep, already covered by EFFECTIVE-389 code-posse — superseded; (2) META-328 filed as a from-scratch coherence advisor specifying mechanical checks that ALREADY SHIP and run every 30 minutes (chump mission-grade INFRA-599, chump roadmap-status INFRA-606/1145 with starved_outcomes + untraced_p0 + --exit-on-drift) — re-scoped down to composition; (3) EFFECTIVE-383 avoided the same fate only by luck, because EFFECTIVE-386 shipped mid-session and someone noticed. All three were caught by a human asking, not by any check. The doctrine does not survive contact with an agent in flow — which is the argument FOR this automation, not against the rule. Note the dedupe must search CODE and SHIPPED COMMANDS too, not just gap titles: META-328's duplicate was a built CLI subcommand, invisible to any title-similarity search.
  opened_date: '2026-08-19'
  outcome_id: ZERO-WASTE-000

